window.SAT_DATA = {"manifest":{"examTitle":"Digital SAT Mock Exam","edition":"International · Hard Calibration","difficultyNote":"Difficulty tags are relative to this exam, which is deliberately calibrated above official College Board difficulty. An [EASY] item here is roughly comparable to an official medium item.","modules":[{"id":"rw1","label":"Reading and Writing · Module 1","section":"Reading and Writing","minutes":32,"questionCount":27,"file":"data/rw-module-1.json","status":"ready","note":"Baseline module. Mixed domains, calibrated above official difficulty."},{"id":"rw2","label":"Reading and Writing · Module 2","section":"Reading and Writing","minutes":32,"questionCount":27,"file":"data/rw-module-2.json","status":"ready","note":"Adaptive mix of difficulties, with 5 questions targeting common international-student error patterns."},{"id":"m1","label":"Math · Module 1","section":"Math","minutes":35,"questionCount":22,"file":"data/math-module-1.json","status":"ready","note":"16 multiple-choice and 6 grid-in questions, with a Desmos calculator and reference sheet."},{"id":"m2","label":"Math · Module 2","section":"Math","minutes":35,"questionCount":22,"file":"data/math-module-2.json","status":"ready","note":"Adaptive mix with 3 multi-step problems built for Desmos."}]},"modules":{"rw1":{"id":"rw1","section":"Reading and Writing","module":1,"title":"Reading and Writing · Module 1","minutes":32,"questions":[{"n":1,"domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"EASY","targetSec":45,"passage":"<p>Marguerite Oyelaran’s field notes describe the alpine beetle as “dogged” in its pursuit of cool microhabitats, but she is careful to add that the word is figurative. The beetle’s movements, she argues, are best understood as ______ responses to temperature gradients: each is triggered by a simple sensory cue rather than by anything resembling foresight or planning.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["meticulous","reflexive","fickle","discretionary"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The passage says each movement is “triggered by a simple sensory cue rather than by anything resembling foresight or planning.” A response that is automatic and unplanned is reflexive.","why":["“Meticulous” means extremely careful and precise, which implies deliberate attention. The passage rules out planning.","Correct. “Reflexive” means automatic and involuntary, matching “triggered by a simple sensory cue.”","“Fickle” means changeable or unreliable. Nothing in the passage says the beetle’s responses vary unpredictably.","“Discretionary” means left to the judgment of the one acting. That is the opposite of a response triggered by a cue."]},{"n":2,"domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":55,"passage":"<p>When the cellist Ayasha Tolgonbaeva premiered her first suite, several critics dismissed it as ______, pointing to its heavy reliance on familiar Central Asian folk melodies. Listeners who returned to the piece years later, however, heard in those same borrowed tunes a subtle process of reworking that made the familiar sound strange.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["derivative","incendiary","cryptic","fastidious"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The critics’ complaint is “heavy reliance on familiar … melodies,” and “however” signals that later listeners saw more originality than the critics did. A work that leans on existing material rather than creating its own is derivative.","why":["Correct. “Derivative” means drawn from earlier sources without much originality, which is what “heavy reliance on familiar … melodies” suggests.","“Incendiary” means inflammatory or provocative. Familiar folk tunes would not make a piece provocative, and the critics’ complaint is about borrowing.","“Cryptic” means obscure or hard to interpret. The melodies are described as familiar, not puzzling.","“Fastidious” means excessively concerned with detail. The passage says nothing about fussiness."]},{"n":3,"domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":75,"passage":"<p>Far from ______, the memoirist’s account of the closure of her family’s textile mill in Bursa treats the mill’s final months with wry detachment. She does not mourn the loss; instead she lingers over absurd details, such as the foreman who insisted on inspecting looms that had already been sold and the accountant who kept balancing the books of a firm that no longer existed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["didactic","sardonic","effusive","elegiac"],"answer":3,"explanation":"“Far from ______” tells you the blank names what the account is NOT. The next sentences say she “does not mourn the loss.” The quality the account lacks is therefore a mournful, lamenting tone, which is “elegiac.”","why":["“Didactic” means intended to teach or instruct. Nothing in the passage contrasts teaching with detachment.","“Sardonic” means bitterly mocking. It is close to what the account IS (wry, detached), but “far from” flips the logic, so the blank must be the opposite quality. This is the tempting trap.","“Effusive” means gushing. The passage contrasts detachment with mourning, not with gushing enthusiasm.","Correct. “Elegiac” means expressing sorrow or lament, exactly what the account is “far from” because “she does not mourn the loss.”"]},{"n":4,"domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":70,"passage":"<p>The advisory panel’s report is careful to ______ its central conclusion: although the data reveal a modest association between the two variables, the authors explicitly decline to claim that either one causes the other.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["corroborate","disseminate","circumscribe","obfuscate"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The authors keep their conclusion within limits by refusing to claim causation. To “circumscribe” a claim is to restrict it within defined boundaries.","why":["“Corroborate” means to confirm with additional evidence. The authors are limiting their claim, not adding support for it.","“Disseminate” means to spread widely. The sentence is about how far the conclusion goes, not how widely it circulates.","Correct. “Circumscribe” means to restrict within limits, matching the authors’ refusal to claim causation.","“Obfuscate” means to make deliberately unclear. The authors are being precise about what the data do and do not show."]},{"n":5,"domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":80,"passage":"<p>Hydrologists who first noticed that certain tropical rivers run the colour of black tea assumed that the tint came chiefly from decaying leaves. In a recent analysis of the Kandara River system, however, the chemist Adaeze Nwosu found that leaf litter accounts for barely a third of the dissolved organic carbon in the river’s tributaries; the remainder leaches from waterlogged, nutrient-poor peat soils in which roots have scavenged for scarce minerals for millennia. Nwosu’s result does not overturn the leaf-litter explanation so much as demote it, from sole cause to one contributor among several.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It reports a widely held explanation, presents a finding that complicates it, and characterizes the significance of that finding.","It states a hypothesis, describes an experiment designed to test it, and reports that the hypothesis was confirmed.","It identifies a puzzle, rejects two proposed solutions, and endorses a third.","It describes a natural phenomenon, criticizes the methods of earlier researchers, and calls for further study."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The first sentence gives the early assumption (leaves), the second gives Nwosu’s complicating finding (only a third from leaves), and the last sentence characterizes what the finding means (it “demotes” rather than “overturns” the old idea).","why":["Correct. Assumption → complicating finding → characterization of its significance.","No experiment is described, and the original explanation is not confirmed. It is qualified.","Only one earlier explanation (leaf litter) is discussed, and it is not rejected outright. No third solution is “endorsed” over two others.","The methods of earlier researchers are never criticized, and no call for further study appears."]},{"n":6,"domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":85,"passage":"<p>Urban planners often admire the Dutch <i>woonerf</i>, a residential street on which cars, cyclists, and pedestrians share a single surface with no kerbs or lane markings. Proponents contend that removing conventional cues forces drivers to slow down and negotiate space with others. <u>Yet in a study of fourteen such streets, the traffic engineer Lieke van Aalst found that average vehicle speeds fell noticeably only on streets shorter than 150 metres.</u> On longer streets, speeds were statistically indistinguishable from those on conventionally marked roads, a pattern that suggests the design’s effect depends less on the absence of markings than on drivers’ perception that a space is small and intimate.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It offers a concrete example that illustrates the proponents’ claim.","It questions the reliability of the study described in the following sentence.","It restates the proponents’ claim in more technical terms.","It introduces evidence that complicates the proponents’ explanation and prepares for an alternative account."],"answer":3,"explanation":"“Yet” signals a turn against the proponents’ view. The underlined sentence gives a finding (the effect appears only on short streets) that the proponents’ explanation cannot easily accommodate, and the final sentence then offers a different account (perceived intimacy).","why":["The sentence does not illustrate the claim. It qualifies it (“Yet … only on streets shorter than 150 metres”).","The following sentence extends the study’s findings; it is not challenged. The passage never doubts the study’s reliability.","The sentence introduces new data. It does not restate anything the proponents said.","Correct. It complicates the proponents’ explanation and sets up the alternative account in the last sentence."]},{"n":7,"domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":100,"passage":"<p><strong>Text 1</strong></p><p>The archaeologist Rahul Menon argues that the abandonment of the hill settlement of Karvela around 1200 CE was driven primarily by prolonged drought. He points to lake-sediment cores from the region showing a marked decline in pollen from cultivated cereals during the period, and he notes that no comparable settlement within thirty kilometres was occupied afterward.</p><p><strong>Text 2</strong></p><p>The historian Sofia Brandão accepts Menon’s sediment evidence but questions his conclusion. Cereal pollen declines, she observes, whenever farmers shift to root crops, which shed little airborne pollen; the sediment record alone therefore cannot distinguish a failing harvest from a change in what was planted. Only direct evidence of food shortage, such as skeletal indicators of malnutrition, would settle the matter.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would Brandão (Text 2) most likely respond to Menon’s use of the sediment cores (Text 1)?","choices":["By arguing that the cores are unreliable because they were drawn from too few locations","By contending that the decline in cereal pollen is compatible with an explanation other than drought","By conceding that drought caused the abandonment but disputing when the drought began","By proposing that the cores be re-examined for evidence of root-crop pollen"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Brandão “accepts” the sediment evidence itself but argues that a decline in cereal pollen could also result from a shift to root crops. She therefore treats the same data as compatible with a non-drought explanation.","why":["Brandão explicitly accepts the sediment evidence. She does not question how many locations were sampled.","Correct. A cereal-pollen decline could also mean farmers switched to root crops, so the evidence does not by itself point to drought.","Brandão does not concede that drought was the cause, and she says nothing about the timing of any drought.","She notes that root crops shed little pollen, so re-examining the cores for it would not help. She recommends skeletal evidence instead."]},{"n":8,"domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":80,"passage":"<p>Development economists have long assumed that microloans succeed chiefly by supplying capital to entrepreneurs who cannot obtain it from banks. A field study led by Nadia Rahman complicates that assumption. Rahman’s team followed 1,200 first-time borrowers across sixty villages, assigning some to repayment groups of five and others to borrow individually on otherwise identical terms. After two years, borrowers in groups reported significantly higher business profits, even though loan sizes did not differ. Interviews suggested why: group members routinely shared supplier contacts, pricing tips, and warnings about unreliable customers. Rahman concludes that the informational advantages of group lending may account for a larger share of microcredit’s benefits than the money itself.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Microloans are less effective for individual borrowers than economists have previously reported.","Repayment groups of five are more likely than larger groups to repay their loans on time.","A study suggests that the benefits of group microloans may stem more from information sharing among borrowers than from the capital provided.","Economists have long underestimated how much capital entrepreneurs in rural villages require."],"answer":2,"explanation":"The text sets up the standard assumption (capital matters most), presents Rahman’s study, and ends with her conclusion that information sharing may matter more than the money. Choice C captures that, including the cautious “may.”","why":["Individual borrowers did earn less than group borrowers, but the text does not say microloans are ineffective for them or that earlier reports overstated their effect.","The study compared groups of five with individual borrowers. It says nothing about repayment rates or larger groups.","Correct. This matches the study design, the interview evidence, and Rahman’s cautious conclusion.","The study shows loan size did not explain the difference in profits. It does not suggest borrowers needed more capital."]},{"n":9,"domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence (Textual)","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":90,"passage":"<p>The ecologist Tomasz Wieczorek hypothesizes that city-dwelling blackbirds sing at higher pitches than their rural counterparts not because urban life as such alters their songs, but because low-frequency traffic noise masks the lower notes of a song, so that individuals singing at higher pitches are more likely to be heard by potential mates.</p>","prompt":"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Wieczorek’s hypothesis?","choices":["The songs of urban blackbirds are, on average, shorter than the songs of rural blackbirds.","Blackbirds in several different cities sing at higher pitches than rural blackbirds do, although the size of the difference varies from city to city.","Blackbirds living in quiet urban parks sing at pitches similar to those of rural blackbirds, while blackbirds living beside busy roads sing at markedly higher pitches.","Rural blackbirds that hear recordings of higher-pitched songs are more likely to approach the source of the recording."],"answer":2,"explanation":"The hypothesis has a specific causal claim: noise, not “urban life as such,” drives the pitch shift. The strongest support separates the two factors. Quiet urban birds sing like rural birds, while birds exposed to traffic noise sing higher.","why":["Song length is irrelevant to pitch and to masking of lower notes.","This is consistent with the hypothesis but equally consistent with urban life “as such” altering songs, which Wieczorek rejects. It does not isolate noise.","Correct. Holding “urban” constant while varying noise isolates noise as the cause.","This concerns rural birds’ response to pitch and does not address traffic noise or masking."]},{"n":10,"domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence (Quantitative)","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":90,"passage":"<p>The botanist Inés Vargas hypothesized that the seeds of wetland plant species are far more sensitive to dry conditions than the seeds of drought-adapted species are. To test this, her team sowed seeds from two drought-adapted species and two wetland species in soil at three moisture levels and recorded the percentage of seeds that germinated within 14 days.</p>","table":{"caption":"Percentage of seeds germinating within 14 days","headers":["Species","Dry soil (10% moisture)","Moist soil (30% moisture)","Saturated soil (50% moisture)"],"rows":[["Species A (drought-adapted)","72","78","41"],["Species B (drought-adapted)","65","69","30"],["Species C (wetland)","12","55","81"],["Species D (wetland)","8","49","77"]]},"prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support Vargas’s hypothesis?","choices":["In saturated soil, wetland species germinated at rates above 75 percent, compared with fewer than 45 percent for the drought-adapted species.","Germination rates for all four species were highest in moist soil.","Species A germinated at a higher rate in dry soil than Species D did in moist soil.","In dry soil, fewer than 15 percent of wetland seeds germinated, whereas at least 65 percent of drought-adapted seeds did."],"answer":3,"explanation":"The hypothesis concerns sensitivity to DRY conditions, so the relevant comparison is germination in dry soil across the two groups: wetland 12% and 8% versus drought-adapted 72% and 65%.","why":["The numbers are accurate but concern saturated soil (waterlogging), not dry conditions. It supports a different claim.","False. Species C and D germinated best in saturated soil (81 and 77), not moist soil.","True (72 vs 49) but compares different species under different moisture levels, so it says nothing about sensitivity to dryness.","Correct. Both wetland species were below 15% (12, 8) and both drought-adapted species were at or above 65% (72, 65) in dry soil."]},{"n":11,"domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Inferences","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":90,"passage":"<p>Because medieval Arabic geographers typically compiled route descriptions from the accounts of merchants and pilgrims rather than from direct survey, they tended to record distances in units of travel time, such as days’ journey, rather than in units of length. The historian Layla Haddad observes that this practice yields a curious result: the distance between two cities as recorded in a single text can differ considerably depending on which route the informant followed, the season of travel, and how heavily laden the caravan was. Haddad therefore concludes that the distances in these texts are best understood as ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["unreliable measurements that were later replaced with surveyed values in the same texts.","deliberate exaggerations meant to convey the hardship of travel.","approximations of the shortest path between cities, expressed in unfamiliar units.","records of particular journeys rather than fixed properties of the places themselves."],"answer":3,"explanation":"The recorded distance depends on the route, season, and load of a specific journey. It is therefore a feature of that journey, not a stable property of the pair of cities.","why":["Nothing in the passage says the values were later replaced or that the texts were revised with surveyed data.","No evidence suggests intent to exaggerate. The variation comes from real differences in journeys.","The passage says the figures vary with route, so they do not represent the shortest path.","Correct. Variation by route, season, and load shows the figures describe individual trips."]},{"n":12,"domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence (Textual)","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":75,"passage":"<p>A student is analyzing Wanjiru Kamau’s novel <i>The Weight of Rain</i>. The student claims that the narrator, Mumbi, reveals her reluctance to accept her father’s decline by absorbing herself in small practical tasks rather than confronting his illness directly.</p>","prompt":"Which quotation from the novel most effectively illustrates the student’s claim?","choices":["“The doctor spoke slowly, as though the words themselves might bruise, and Mumbi nodded without hearing any of them.”","“Outside, the rains had come early, and the roof of the clinic drummed with a sound like distant applause.”","“Each morning she counted his pills into the blue dish, lining them up by size, and each morning she told the nurse that he had slept well.”","“Her brother arrived from Nairobi that evening, and the two of them sat in the kitchen without speaking until the kettle went cold.”"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The claim has two parts: (1) she busies herself with small practical tasks and (2) she avoids confronting the illness. Only choice C shows both: the ritual of counting pills and the routine reassurance that he “had slept well.”","why":["Shows Mumbi not listening (avoidance) but no practical tasks.","Describes the setting and says nothing about Mumbi’s behavior.","Correct. Counting pills into a dish is a small practical task, and telling the nurse he slept well avoids the reality of his decline.","Shows silence between siblings but no practical task or avoidance of the illness."]},{"n":13,"domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence (Quantitative)","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":100,"passage":"<p>A housing researcher claims that although rooftop solar adoption has grown in every income group, it has grown fastest in proportional terms among the lowest-income households, even as the absolute gap between the highest- and lowest-income households has widened.</p>","chart":{"title":"Share of households with rooftop solar panels, by income group","yLabel":"Households with panels (%)","categories":["Lowest-income third","Middle third","Highest-income third"],"series":[{"name":"2015","values":[2,4,11]},{"name":"2023","values":[9,18,27]}]},"prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to illustrate the researcher’s claim?","choices":["Between 2015 and 2023, adoption among lowest-income households rose about 4.5-fold (from 2 to 9 percent) while adoption among highest-income households rose about 2.5-fold (from 11 to 27 percent); over the same period, the gap between the two groups grew from 9 to 18 percentage points.","Between 2015 and 2023, adoption among highest-income households rose about 4.5-fold (from 2 to 9 percent) while adoption among lowest-income households rose about 2.5-fold (from 11 to 27 percent); over the same period, the gap between the two groups narrowed from 18 to 9 percentage points.","Adoption among lowest-income households rose by 7 percentage points and among highest-income households by 16 percentage points, so proportional growth was greatest among the highest-income households.","Adoption among lowest- and middle-income households each rose about 4.5-fold, and the gap between middle- and highest-income households widened from 7 to 9 percentage points."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Two things must be shown: proportional growth was greatest for the lowest group (2→9 is 4.5×, versus 11→27 at about 2.5×) and the absolute gap widened (11−2 = 9 points in 2015; 27−9 = 18 points in 2023). Only choice A does both accurately.","why":["Correct. Lowest: 2→9 = 4.5×. Highest: 11→27 ≈ 2.5×. Gap: 9 points (2015) → 18 points (2023).","The data are assigned to the wrong groups and the gap is described as narrowing from 18 to 9, which reverses the true direction.","The point increases are accurate (7 and 16), but the conclusion is wrong. Proportionally, 2→9 grew far more than 11→27. It also ignores the gap claim.","The statistics are accurate (4→18 is 4.5×; gap 7→9), but the choice ignores the highest-versus-lowest comparison the researcher makes."]},{"n":14,"domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Inferences","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":85,"passage":"<p>Physicists modelling heat flow through a novel ceramic insulation predicted that thicker layers would always insulate better. Experiments by Kaito Nishimura’s team, however, showed that beyond a thickness of 4 centimetres additional material produced no measurable improvement, and beyond 6 centimetres performance worsened slightly. Nishimura suggests that in thicker layers the ceramic cools unevenly during manufacture, forming internal cracks that allow air to circulate within the layer. If Nishimura’s explanation is correct, a manufacturer seeking the best insulation from this ceramic would most likely ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["apply the ceramic in layers no thicker than about 4 centimetres, adding further layers only if more insulation is needed.","use the thickest layers its process can produce, since thickness reliably improves insulation.","abandon the ceramic entirely, since its insulating ability declines as thickness increases.","choose layers of about 6 centimetres, since that thickness produced the best insulation in the experiments."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Insulation stopped improving at about 4 cm, and Nishimura attributes the later decline to cracks in thicker layers. Keeping each layer to about 4 cm avoids that problem while still allowing more total insulation through additional layers.","why":["Correct. This uses the ceramic at its effective thickness and avoids the cracking mechanism.","Contradicts the experiments: beyond 4 cm there was no gain, and beyond 6 cm performance worsened.","Overreach. The ceramic performs well up to about 4 cm. The problem occurs only in thicker layers.","Misreads the data. Performance did not improve past 4 cm and worsened past 6 cm."]},{"n":15,"domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":40,"passage":"<p>The glaciologist Ana Sofía Reyes, who has spent eleven austral winters drilling ice cores on a high plateau in southern Patagonia ______ argues that the record preserved there is unusually fine-grained: individual storms can be distinguished layer by layer.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["Patagonia;","Patagonia,","Patagonia:","Patagonia"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“who has spent … Patagonia” is a nonessential relative clause that interrupts the subject and its verb (“Reyes … argues”). It began after a comma (“Reyes,”) so it must end with a matching comma.","why":["A semicolon must separate two independent clauses. Here it would separate a subject from its verb.","Correct. The comma closes the nonessential clause opened by the comma after “Reyes.”","A colon must follow an independent clause. “Reyes, who has spent … Patagonia” is not one.","Without punctuation the nonessential clause is left open, since it began with a comma."]},{"n":16,"domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":45,"passage":"<p>The trial’s investigators had anticipated that the compound would relieve symptoms in the treatment group ______ participants given the compound reported symptoms no different from those of participants given the placebo.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["group; instead, participants","group, instead, participants","group; instead participants","group instead: participants"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Two independent clauses are joined here, and “instead” is a conjunctive adverb. The correct pattern is a semicolon before it and a comma after it.","why":["Correct. Semicolon + conjunctive adverb + comma.","The commas create a comma splice, since a comma alone cannot join two independent clauses.","The semicolon is right, but the conjunctive adverb “instead” needs a comma after it.","Creates a run-on, and a colon is inappropriate because the second clause contrasts with, rather than explains, the first."]},{"n":17,"domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":45,"passage":"<p>Coral bleaching—the expulsion of the symbiotic algae that give reef-building corals both their colour and much of their nutrition ______ occurs when sea temperatures remain even one or two degrees above the seasonal maximum for several weeks.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["nutrition,","nutrition—","nutrition;","nutrition:"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The sentence opens the interrupting phrase with a dash after “bleaching,” so the phrase must be closed with a dash before the verb “occurs.” Pairs of dashes must match.","why":["Mixing a dash with a comma is not allowed.","Correct. The closing dash completes the pair opened after “bleaching.”","A semicolon would separate the subject phrase from its verb.","A colon would separate the subject phrase from its verb and does not close the dash-set-off phrase."]},{"n":18,"domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":40,"passage":"<p>Among the artifacts now displayed in the museum’s new maritime gallery ______ a set of bronze dividers, whose finely cut gradations suggest that the ship’s navigator relied on more than pilots’ rules of thumb.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["are","were","was","is"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The sentence is inverted: the real subject is “a set of bronze dividers” (singular), not “artifacts.” The surrounding verbs (“displayed,” “suggest”) are present tense, so the verb must be singular and present: “is.”","why":["“Are” is plural and would agree with “artifacts,” which is only the object of the preposition “among.”","“Were” is plural and past tense.","“Was” agrees in number but is past tense. The context (“now displayed,” “suggest”) is present.","Correct. Singular subject (“a set”), present tense."]},{"n":19,"domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":50,"passage":"<p>Excavated in the 1960s and initially assumed to be purely decorative, ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["scholars have since shown that the glazed tiles encode records of trade routes.","records of trade routes have since been shown by scholars to be encoded in the glazed tiles.","the glazed tiles have since been shown by scholars to encode records of trade routes.","it has since been shown by scholars that the glazed tiles encode records of trade routes."],"answer":2,"explanation":"The opening modifier (“Excavated … and initially assumed to be purely decorative”) must describe the noun that comes immediately after the comma. Only the tiles were excavated and assumed to be decorative.","why":["Dangling modifier. It implies the scholars were excavated and assumed decorative.","Dangling modifier. It implies the records of trade routes were excavated and assumed decorative.","Correct. “The glazed tiles” is the logical noun to be modified.","“It” is an expletive with no logical referent for the modifier, so the modifier dangles."]},{"n":20,"domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"EASY","targetSec":35,"passage":"<p>If the reservoir’s operators ______ its storage capacity two decades ago, the city would not now be rationing water each summer.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["expanded","had expanded","would have expanded","have expanded"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The sentence is a counterfactual conditional about the past (“two decades ago”). The “if” clause takes the past perfect (“had expanded”), and the main clause takes “would” + verb.","why":["Simple past is used for present or future unreal conditions, not for an unreal past event.","Correct. “If + past perfect … would not now be” is the standard mixed conditional.","“Would have” belongs in the result clause, not in the “if” clause.","Present perfect conflicts with “two decades ago,” which names a completed time in the past."]},{"n":21,"domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"EASY","targetSec":40,"passage":"<p>Lichens are often described as single organisms, yet each is in fact a partnership between a fungus and at least one photosynthetic partner. ______ the fungus supplies structure and retains moisture, while the alga or cyanobacterium supplies sugars produced from sunlight.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Nevertheless,","Consequently,","Specifically,","Likewise,"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The second sentence spells out what each partner contributes, which specifies the “partnership” introduced in the first sentence.","why":["“Nevertheless” signals contrast. The second sentence elaborates instead.","“Consequently” signals a result. The partners’ roles are not a result of the partnership.","Correct. “Specifically” introduces detail that clarifies the general statement.","“Likewise” signals similarity between two separate points. Here there is one point elaborated."]},{"n":22,"domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":55,"passage":"<p>The company’s 2022 report described its pilot programme as a success, citing a 14 percent rise in customer sign-ups. ______ the same report acknowledged that only 3 percent of the new customers were still active six months later, a figure that complicates the report’s optimistic framing.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Nevertheless,","Thus,","Furthermore,","Likewise,"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The first sentence gives an optimistic claim and the second a fact that undercuts it. The relationship is one of contrast or concession.","why":["Correct. “Nevertheless” marks a contrast: despite the success claim, the report admits a weakness.","“Thus” signals a result. The low retention does not follow from the success claim.","“Furthermore” adds a supporting point, but the second sentence weakens the first.","“Likewise” signals similarity, but the two sentences point in opposite directions."]},{"n":23,"domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":60,"passage":"<p>Pollen counts are commonly reported as though they were a direct measure of allergy risk. ______ the relationship is loose: a day with modest counts of a highly allergenic grass pollen can provoke more symptoms than a day with high counts of a mild one.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Thus,","Likewise,","Meanwhile,","In fact,"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The first sentence describes a common assumption, and the second corrects it by stating what is actually the case. “In fact” introduces that correction.","why":["“Thus” signals a result or conclusion, but the second sentence corrects the first.","“Likewise” signals similarity, but the second sentence contradicts the first.","“Meanwhile” signals simultaneous events, which is not the relationship.","Correct. “In fact” introduces the true state of affairs against the common assumption."]},{"n":24,"domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":75,"passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Solano Institute compared two irrigation methods on tomato farms in a semi-arid region.</li><li>Drip irrigation used an average of 4,200 cubic metres of water per hectare per season.</li><li>Flood irrigation used an average of 9,800 cubic metres of water per hectare per season.</li><li>Yields were nearly identical: 71 tonnes per hectare under drip irrigation and 70 tonnes per hectare under flood irrigation.</li></ul>","prompt":"The student wants to emphasize that drip irrigation achieved comparable yields while using far less water. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["In the Solano Institute’s study, drip irrigation used 4,200 cubic metres of water per hectare per season, and flood irrigation used 9,800.","Although tomato yields were nearly identical (71 tonnes per hectare under drip irrigation versus 70 under flood), drip irrigation used 4,200 cubic metres of water per hectare per season, less than half the 9,800 that flood irrigation used.","Drip irrigation produced 71 tonnes of tomatoes per hectare, slightly more than the 70 tonnes per hectare produced under flood irrigation.","Because flood irrigation used more than twice as much water as drip irrigation, it produced a higher tomato yield."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The goal requires both parts: comparable yields and much lower water use. Only choice B states both. It also uses accurate quantities (4,200 is less than half of 9,800).","why":["States the water figures but omits yield, so it cannot show that yields were comparable.","Correct. Contains both facts with accurate figures and an accurate comparison.","States yield only, so it omits the water savings.","Contradicts the notes. Flood irrigation’s yield (70) was slightly lower, not higher."]},{"n":25,"domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":75,"passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>In 2020, linguist Chukwuemeka Obi published a study of code-switching, the alternation between two languages within a conversation.</li><li>Obi recorded 48 Yoruba–English bilingual speakers in Lagos during casual conversations.</li><li>Each recording lasted about 40 minutes.</li><li>Speakers switched languages most often at clause boundaries rather than in the middle of a clause.</li></ul>","prompt":"The student wants to introduce Obi’s study and its main finding to an audience unfamiliar with the topic. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["In a 2020 study of code-switching, linguist Chukwuemeka Obi recorded 48 Yoruba–English bilingual speakers in casual conversation in Lagos and found that they switched languages most often at clause boundaries rather than mid-clause.","Speakers who use two languages, such as the 48 Yoruba–English bilinguals recorded in Lagos, tend to switch at clause boundaries.","Linguist Chukwuemeka Obi has spent years studying how people who speak more than one language behave in conversation.","Obi’s 2020 study consisted of 48 recordings, each lasting about 40 minutes."],"answer":0,"explanation":"An introduction to an unfamiliar audience should name the researcher, the topic, the method, and the finding. Choice A does all four and stays within the notes.","why":["Correct. Names the researcher and topic, describes the method, and states the finding.","Omits the researcher and the study, and generalizes from a single sample of 48 to speakers in general.","“Has spent years” is not in the notes, and the sentence contains no study details or finding.","Gives method details only and omits the finding."]},{"n":26,"domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":80,"passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>A qanat is an underground channel that carries groundwater from an upland aquifer to lower-lying farmland.</li><li>Qanats have been used in Iran for more than 2,500 years.</li><li>Some Iranian qanats are more than 70 kilometres long.</li><li>Because qanats rely on gravity alone, they need no pumps or external energy.</li></ul>","prompt":"The student wants to explain why qanats are regarded as an efficient technology. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["An underground channel that carries groundwater to farmland, a qanat can be more than 70 kilometres long.","Some qanats, more than 70 kilometres long, have carried groundwater by gravity alone for more than 2,500 years.","Qanats, which have been used in Iran for more than 2,500 years, carry groundwater downhill by gravity alone and so require no pumps or external energy.","Qanats, some of which are more than 70 kilometres long, are the oldest surviving irrigation systems in the world."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Efficiency is explained by the fact that qanats “rely on gravity alone” and so “need no pumps or external energy.” Choice C states this and adds the supporting longevity fact without altering it.","why":["Describes what a qanat is and how long one can be, but gives no reason for efficiency.","Merges two separate facts into an unsupported claim: the notes do not say that 70-kilometre qanats have operated for 2,500 years.","Correct. Gives the efficiency reason (gravity, no pumps or external energy) and stays within the notes.","“The oldest surviving irrigation systems in the world” is not in the notes, and the sentence does not address efficiency."]},{"n":27,"domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":85,"passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Researchers surveyed 2,000 adults in 2022 about their daily commute times.</li><li>Respondents were recruited through a smartphone app that tracks commutes.</li><li>The average reported commute was 38 minutes.</li><li>The researchers note that people who use commute-tracking apps may be more concerned with efficiency than most commuters are.</li></ul>","prompt":"The student wants to present the survey’s main finding while noting a limitation on how far it can be generalized. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The 2022 survey of 2,000 adults found an average commute of 38 minutes.","Although 2,000 adults were surveyed, the average commute of 38 minutes shows that most commuters spend too long travelling.","Researchers recruited respondents through an app designed to track commutes, a method common in modern surveys.","Although the 2022 survey of 2,000 adults found an average commute of 38 minutes, its respondents were recruited through a commute-tracking app and may be more concerned with efficiency than most commuters are."],"answer":3,"explanation":"The goal has two parts: (1) the main finding and (2) a limitation on generalizing it. Choice D reports the 38-minute average and then flags the recruitment method that could make respondents unrepresentative.","why":["States the finding but gives no limitation.","“Shows that most commuters spend too long travelling” is an unsupported judgment, and no limitation is noted.","Describes the recruitment method but omits the finding, and “common in modern surveys” is not in the notes.","Correct. Gives the finding and the limitation, staying within the notes."]}]},"rw2":{"id":"rw2","section":"Reading and Writing","module":2,"title":"Reading and Writing · Module 2","minutes":32,"questions":[{"n":1,"domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":65,"passage":"<p>Although the mayor’s speech was widely praised for its eloquence, the urban planner Kofi Mensah found its promises ______: every commitment was worded so loosely that no future outcome could be counted as a failure to keep it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["exorbitant","audacious","equivocal","sanguine"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The colon introduces the explanation: each commitment was worded loosely enough that any outcome could be defended. Promises open to more than one interpretation are equivocal.","why":["“Exorbitant” means excessive, usually of a price or demand. Loosely worded promises are not excessive.","“Audacious” means bold. Vague commitments are the opposite of bold ones, since nothing is risked.","Correct. “Equivocal” means open to more than one interpretation, which is why no outcome could count as a broken promise.","“Sanguine” means optimistic. The planner’s complaint is about vagueness, not about optimism."]},{"n":2,"domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":60,"intl":"Near-synonym connotation (impartial vs. apathetic)","passage":"<p>The botanist’s tone in her letter was scrupulously ______: she neither praised nor condemned her rival’s hypothesis, reporting only which of its predictions the field data had and had not borne out.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["impartial","apathetic","ambivalent","evasive"],"answer":0,"explanation":"She takes no side and simply reports the evidence, which describes an impartial tone. The other words all describe a neutral-looking attitude but carry a different meaning.","why":["Correct. “Impartial” means not favouring either side, matching “neither praised nor condemned.”","“Apathetic” means lacking interest or concern. Careful reporting of which predictions held shows she cared about the result. Learners often treat “neutral” and “uninterested” as synonyms.","“Ambivalent” means torn between conflicting feelings. Nothing suggests she has mixed feelings.","“Evasive” means avoiding a direct answer. She reports specific results directly."]},{"n":3,"domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":75,"passage":"<p>Critics have called the project’s environmental review ______ on the question that matters most: although the document runs to nine hundred pages, it treats the likely loss of downstream fisheries in a single paragraph.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["exhaustive","prescient","indiscriminate","perfunctory"],"answer":3,"explanation":"“Although” sets up a contrast between the review’s length and its treatment of the key issue. Dealing with the most consequential effect in one paragraph is a token, minimal effort, which is what perfunctory means.","why":["“Exhaustive” means thorough. Length might suggest it, but a single paragraph on the key issue is not thorough.","“Prescient” means accurately foreseeing the future. The passage does not concern whether the review predicted anything.","“Indiscriminate” means not selective. The length tempts you to choose it, but the criticism is about too little attention to the most important effect, not about a lack of selectivity.","Correct. “Perfunctory” means carried out with minimal effort, matching a single paragraph on the most consequential effect."]},{"n":4,"domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":80,"passage":"<p>In the opening stanzas of Halima Idrissi’s poem “Ferry Timetable,” the speaker catalogues the departures of a Mediterranean ferry with bureaucratic precision: 06:40, 09:15, 13:50. The tone is so flat that a reader might mistake the poem for a found document. Only in the final stanza does the register break, when the speaker mentions that the 13:50 sailing is the one on which her brother left and did not return. Read again in that light, the earlier listing of times becomes an act of restraint, a grief held in check by the very form that seems to exclude it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?","choices":["To argue that the poem’s formal restraint is a failure to express emotion","To explain how the poem’s flat opening takes on emotional significance once its final stanza is understood","To compare the poem with official documents such as ferry timetables","To criticize readers who mistake the poem for a found document"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The text describes the flat opening, notes the break in the final stanza, and then explains that the opening “becomes an act of restraint” in retrospect. Its purpose is to show how the meaning of the opening changes.","why":["The text says the restraint is meaningful (“grief held in check”), not a failure.","Correct. The passage traces how the opening is reinterpreted after the final stanza.","The timetable form is mentioned, but the text does not compare the poem with real documents.","The reader’s possible mistake is noted neutrally. The text does not criticize anyone."]},{"n":5,"domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":100,"passage":"<p><strong>Text 1</strong></p><p>The economist Daniela Ortiz argues that fully remote working depresses innovation. Analyzing patent filings from 300 firms, she found that firms that moved to fully remote operations filed 8 percent fewer patents over the following two years than comparable firms that did not, and she attributes the gap to the loss of spontaneous exchanges among engineers who no longer share a workplace.</p><p><strong>Text 2</strong></p><p>The organizational researcher Thabo Nkosi observes that Ortiz’s comparison group consisted mainly of firms in sectors where most staff already lived near company campuses. The firms that went fully remote were therefore disproportionately those whose staff were being relocated or reduced. If so, the fall in patents may reflect disruption caused by restructuring rather than remoteness itself.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would Nkosi (Text 2) most likely characterize Ortiz’s explanation for the decline in patents (Text 1)?","choices":["As accurate but incomplete, because it overlooks the effect of remote work on team size","As insufficiently supported, because the firms compared may have differed in ways that could account for the decline","As correct about the cause but wrong about how large the effect was","As irrelevant, because patent filings are a poor measure of innovation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Nkosi does not dispute the 8 percent gap. He argues that the firms differed in another important way (restructuring), so the gap may not be caused by remoteness. That makes Ortiz’s explanation insufficiently supported.","why":["Nkosi does not mention team size, and he does not call the explanation accurate.","Correct. Nkosi points to a difference between the groups (restructuring) that could explain the decline.","Nkosi does not accept the cause, and he does not discuss the size of the effect.","Nkosi never questions patents as a measure. His objection concerns how the firms were compared."]},{"n":6,"domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":85,"passage":"<p>When the astronomer Mei-Ling Zhao’s team examined light from a distant galaxy cluster, they expected the sharp brightening and steady fade of a typical supernova. <u>Instead they recorded a slow, symmetrical rise in brightness followed by an equally slow decline, a signature that no existing model of stellar explosion predicts.</u> Zhao cautions that the observation was made with a single instrument and may yet prove to be an artefact. If it is confirmed, however, it would imply that at least one kind of stellar death proceeds by a mechanism astronomers have not yet described.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It presents an unexpected observation that prompts the discussion of what the finding could mean.","It describes the method by which the team ruled out an instrument error.","It defines a technical term that the rest of the text relies on.","It rebuts the caution Zhao expresses in the following sentence."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The sentence reports what the team actually saw (“Instead …”), which contradicts their expectation. The rest of the text then discusses the caution about the result and its possible implications.","why":["Correct. The unexpected observation is the basis for the caution and the implication that follow.","The text says the instrument error has not been ruled out, so no such method is described.","No term is defined in the sentence.","The caution follows the sentence and is not rebutted by it. The next sentence expresses doubt about the observation."]},{"n":7,"domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":60,"passage":"<p>Because her data came from only two coastal villages, the anthropologist was ______ about extending her conclusions to the whole region, hedging each claim with an explicit note on its limits.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["credulous","dogmatic","blithe","circumspect"],"answer":3,"explanation":"She hedges every claim and states its limits, which describes careful consideration of the risks of overgeneralizing. That is what circumspect means.","why":["“Credulous” means too ready to believe things. Hedging every claim shows the opposite.","“Dogmatic” means asserting opinions as certain. She qualifies each claim.","“Blithe” means casually unconcerned. She is clearly concerned about the limits of her data.","Correct. “Circumspect” means cautious and mindful of possible consequences."]},{"n":8,"domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":80,"passage":"<p>Historians of the Silk Roads once emphasized caravans carrying luxury goods across thousands of kilometres. Recent work by Ilyas Bekov on customs registers from markets around Samarkand suggests a different picture: the great majority of recorded transactions involved bulk goods such as grain, salt, and timber moving fewer than 200 kilometres. Long-distance luxuries, though vivid in travellers’ accounts, made up only a small share of trade by volume. Bekov does not deny their cultural influence; he argues only that a picture of the routes built from luxuries alone mistakes the memorable for the typical.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Luxury goods carried along the Silk Roads had little cultural influence.","Customs registers are more reliable than travellers’ accounts for every historical question.","Bekov’s analysis of market records suggests that everyday regional trade in bulk goods, rather than long-distance luxuries, made up most Silk Road commerce.","Travellers in the region tended to exaggerate the distances that caravans covered."],"answer":2,"explanation":"The text sets the older emphasis on luxuries against Bekov’s finding that short-distance bulk trade dominated by volume. Choice C states this without overstating it.","why":["The text says Bekov “does not deny their cultural influence.”","The text says nothing about registers being more reliable in general.","Correct. This matches Bekov’s finding and its contrast with the earlier view.","Travellers’ accounts are called vivid, not exaggerated, and distances are not disputed."]},{"n":9,"domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence (Quantitative)","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":100,"passage":"<p>A transport analyst claims that cities that added more protected cycle lanes saw larger gains in cycling’s share of commutes, but that adding lanes did not by itself guarantee a high share, because starting conditions also mattered.</p>","table":{"caption":"Protected cycle lanes added and bicycle share of commutes in four cities","headers":["City","Protected lanes added, 2015–2022 (km)","Bicycle share of commutes, 2015 (%)","Bicycle share of commutes, 2022 (%)"],"rows":[["City P","84","6","14"],["City Q","61","3","7"],["City R","22","7","9"],["City S","9","8","8"]]},"prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the analyst’s claim?","choices":["City P added the most protected lanes (84 km), and its bicycle share more than doubled, from 6 percent to 14 percent.","Bicycle share rose in every city between 2015 and 2022.","City S, which added the fewest protected lanes, was the only city in which bicycle share fell.","City Q added nearly three times as many kilometres of protected lanes as City R (61 versus 22) and gained more in bicycle share (4 versus 2 percentage points), yet City R’s 2022 share (9 percent) was still higher than City Q’s (7 percent)."],"answer":3,"explanation":"The claim has two parts: more lanes means a larger gain, and lanes alone do not guarantee a high share. Only choice D covers both, using the comparison of City Q and City R.","why":["The figures are accurate (6 to 14 is more than double) but support only the first part of the claim.","False. City S stayed at 8 percent.","False. City S’s share did not fall; it stayed at 8 percent.","Correct. 61 km is nearly three times 22 km; gains were +4 and +2 percentage points; and 2022 shares were 7 and 9 percent. This shows both parts of the claim."]},{"n":10,"domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence (Textual)","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":80,"passage":"<p>The marine biologist Leila Haddad proposes that the bold yellow stripes of a small reef fish, the ember goby, warn predators that the fish tastes unpleasant, a strategy known as warning colouration. Under this proposal, predators that have sampled ember gobies should learn to avoid them.</p>","prompt":"Which finding, if true, would most directly undermine Haddad’s proposal?","choices":["Predatory fish that had previously eaten ember gobies attacked them just as often as fish that had never encountered them did, and swallowed them without apparent distress.","Ember gobies are more abundant on reefs that have fewer predatory fish.","The yellow stripes of ember gobies are brighter during the breeding season than at other times of year.","Several other reef fish with similar yellow stripes also taste unpleasant to predators."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The proposal predicts that predators with experience of ember gobies will avoid them. If experienced predators attack just as often and show no distaste, the prediction fails.","why":["Correct. It directly contradicts the prediction that experience leads to avoidance.","Abundance patterns could have many causes and do not test whether the stripes warn predators.","Seasonal brightness is unrelated to whether predators learn to avoid the fish.","This would, if anything, fit a warning-colouration strategy shared among species. It does not undermine the proposal."]},{"n":11,"domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Inferences","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":90,"passage":"<p>The hydrologist Priya Nair observes that in the tributaries of a mountain river, dry-season flow is closely proportional to the area of glacier upstream, whereas wet-season flow shows no relationship to glacier area because monsoon rain dominates. Glacier area across the basin has shrunk by about a fifth over thirty years. On the basis of Nair’s observation, it is most reasonable to infer that over those thirty years ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["wet-season flow has fallen by roughly a fifth.","dry-season flow has probably fallen, while wet-season flow may have been unaffected by glacier loss.","neither dry-season nor wet-season flow has changed.","dry-season flow has risen because shrinking glaciers release more meltwater."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Dry-season flow tracks glacier area, so a smaller glacier area implies lower dry-season flow. Wet-season flow is unrelated to glacier area, so glacier loss gives no reason to expect a change in it.","why":["Wet-season flow is unrelated to glacier area, so there is no basis for a fifth fall.","Correct. The proportionality supports a fall in dry-season flow, and the lack of relationship means wet-season flow may be unaffected.","Dry-season flow is proportional to glacier area, so a fifth less glacier makes “no change” unlikely.","The passage gives no support for a rise. Dry-season flow is proportional to glacier area, which has shrunk."]},{"n":12,"domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence (Textual)","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":85,"passage":"<p>A student is analyzing Cheng Yao’s short story “The Lamp Repairer.” The student claims that the protagonist, Wen, measures his self-worth by whether his skills are useful to others rather than by whether others recognize him.</p>","prompt":"Which quotation from the story most effectively illustrates the student’s claim?","choices":["“The neighbours nodded to him in the market, and he nodded back, as one does.”","“When the shop closed, Wen went on mending broken lamps at his kitchen table, though no one came to collect them.”","“Wen did not care that the mayor had praised him at the festival; what troubled him was that three lamps still sat unmended on his bench when the evening ended.”","“At the festival the mayor praised him by name, and Wen studied his shoes.”"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The claim has two parts: usefulness matters to Wen, and recognition does not. Only choice C shows both: he does not care about the mayor’s praise, and he is troubled by unmended lamps that could be of use.","why":["Shows an ordinary social exchange and says nothing about Wen’s sense of worth.","Shows Wen mending lamps, but no one collects them, so it does not show that he values being useful to others.","Correct. Recognition (the mayor’s praise) does not matter to him; unfinished, useful work troubles him.","Shows discomfort with recognition but says nothing about usefulness."]},{"n":13,"domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"EASY","targetSec":75,"passage":"<p>When the astronomer Yusuf al-Rami built an observatory at Tarsalan in 1481, he equipped it with instruments more precise than any then available in Europe. Yet the building was demolished within three years. Chroniclers blamed religious hostility toward astronomy, and later historians repeated the explanation. Nuran Aydin’s reading of the court documents suggests a more mundane cause: the observatory had been founded under the patronage of a single sultan, and when a plague and a military defeat emptied the treasury and shifted courtly favour to that sultan’s rivals, the institution lost its protector. On this account, the observatory fell not because its work was condemned but because its patron’s influence had waned.</p>","prompt":"According to the text, what does Aydin’s reading of the court documents suggest about the destruction of the observatory?","choices":["It resulted from religious objections to astronomy voiced at court.","It occurred because the observatory’s instruments proved less accurate than those in Europe.","It followed the decline in influence of the sultan who had sponsored the observatory.","It was ordered to raise funds for the treasury after the plague."],"answer":2,"explanation":"The text says the observatory “lost its protector” when the sultan’s influence waned, and that it fell “not because its work was condemned.”","why":["This is the older explanation that Aydin’s reading replaces.","The instruments are described as more precise than European ones.","Correct. The sponsor’s waning influence is Aydin’s proposed cause.","The plague drained the treasury, but the text does not say the demolition was meant to raise funds."]},{"n":14,"domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Inferences","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":85,"passage":"<p>In a study of 2,400 university students, those who took handwritten notes scored higher on conceptual questions than those who typed notes on laptops, although the two groups performed equally well on questions of factual recall. The researchers propose that handwriting is slower, which forces students to summarize a lecture rather than transcribe it, and that summarizing deepens understanding. If the researchers’ proposal is correct, a laptop user who wanted to close the gap on conceptual questions would most likely benefit from ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["putting each idea into their own words instead of typing what the lecturer says word for word.","typing faster so that more of the lecture is captured.","switching off the laptop’s notifications during the lecture.","studying the notes again immediately after the lecture."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The proposed mechanism is summarizing rather than transcribing. A typist who deliberately paraphrases would reproduce that mechanism.","why":["Correct. Paraphrasing imitates the effect that slower handwriting is proposed to cause.","Typing faster would increase transcription, which is the opposite of what the proposal recommends.","Notifications are not part of the proposed mechanism.","Re-studying may help in general, but the proposal is about how notes are made, not how they are reviewed."]},{"n":15,"domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":40,"passage":"<p>The expedition’s three objectives were ambitious ______ to map the glacier’s retreat since 1990, to sample the meltwater lake beneath its tongue, and to test whether the drilling equipment could operate at −40 degrees Celsius.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["ambitious,","ambitious;","ambitious and","ambitious:"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The words before the blank form a complete sentence, and what follows is a list that specifies the “three objectives.” A colon correctly introduces such a list.","why":["A comma cannot join a complete sentence to a list that explains it; this creates a splice.","A semicolon must join two independent clauses. The list is not one.","“And” makes the infinitive list ungrammatical (“ambitious and to map …”).","Correct. A colon follows an independent clause and introduces the list that explains it."]},{"n":16,"domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":35,"intl":"Preposition collocation (conducive to)","passage":"<p>Rapid urbanization is not necessarily conducive ______ the preservation of local languages, since children in dense cities tend to adopt the dominant regional tongue.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["for","to","with","of"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The adjective “conducive” is always followed by “to” (conducive to something).","why":["“Conducive for” is a common learner error. The fixed collocation is “conducive to.”","Correct. “Conducive to” is the standard idiom.","“Conducive with” is not a standard construction.","“Conducive of” is not idiomatic in modern English."]},{"n":17,"domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":45,"passage":"<p>Field teams were sent to Almaty, Kazakhstan ______ Tbilisi, Georgia; and Yerevan, Armenia, where they interviewed residents about household water use.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["Kazakhstan;","Kazakhstan,","Kazakhstan:","Kazakhstan and"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Each item in the series (city, country) already contains a comma. When items contain internal commas, semicolons separate them, as in the later “Tbilisi, Georgia; and Yerevan, Armenia.”","why":["Correct. A semicolon separates list items that contain commas.","A comma would make it unclear where one city ends and the next begins and is inconsistent with the later semicolon.","A colon is not used between items in a series.","“And” here, together with the later “; and,” would create an inconsistent series."]},{"n":18,"domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"EASY","targetSec":30,"intl":"Countable vs. uncountable quantity words (number/amount)","passage":"<p>Although the trial enrolled a larger ______ of participants than earlier studies did, the amount of data collected from each participant was smaller.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["amount","quantity","number","portion"],"answer":2,"explanation":"“Participants” are countable, so the quantity noun that fits is “number.” “Amount” is used for uncountable things such as “data,” which is why the sentence uses it later.","why":["“Amount” is for uncountable quantities. People are counted, so “amount of participants” is incorrect.","“Quantity” is used mainly for uncountable or measured amounts and reads awkwardly with people.","Correct. “Number” is used with countable plural nouns.","“Portion” means a part of a whole and does not fit “a larger ___ of participants.”"]},{"n":19,"domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":40,"passage":"<p>The archive holds several manuscripts ______ authenticity scholars have long disputed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["who","that","which","whose"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The blank must link “manuscripts” to “authenticity” with a possessive meaning (the manuscripts’ authenticity). “Whose” does this and can refer to things as well as people.","why":["“Who” refers to people and is not possessive.","“That” cannot show possession, so “that authenticity” leaves the sentence without a link between the two nouns.","“Which” is not possessive. “Of which” would be needed.","Correct. “Whose authenticity” means “the manuscripts’ authenticity.”"]},{"n":20,"domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":35,"intl":"Unnecessary punctuation after a verb","passage":"<p>The laboratory’s three main instruments include ______ a mass spectrometer, a confocal microscope, and a cryostat.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["include:","include","include,","include;"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A list that completes the verb (“include …”) needs no punctuation between the verb and its objects. A colon, comma, or semicolon there would separate the verb from what it governs.","why":["A colon must follow a complete sentence. “Instruments include” is not one.","Correct. No punctuation is needed between the verb and its objects.","A comma would separate the verb from its objects.","A semicolon must join independent clauses and cannot follow a verb."]},{"n":21,"domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":55,"passage":"<p>Early reviewers praised the film’s cinematography but found its plot thin. ______ the film has since attracted a devoted audience, many of whom cite the very sparseness of the plot as the source of its appeal.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Even so,","For this reason,","Similarly,","In other words,"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The second sentence describes a positive outcome that survives the early criticism, so it needs a concessive transition.","why":["Correct. “Even so” acknowledges the criticism and introduces a contrasting outcome.","“For this reason” would mean the thin plot caused the audience, which the text does not say.","“Similarly” signals a parallel, but the sentences contrast.","“In other words” would restate the first sentence, which the second does not do."]},{"n":22,"domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":65,"passage":"<p>Proponents of the four-day working week point to trials in which output held steady while absences fell. Critics reply that such trials attract firms that were already inclined to succeed. ______ the strongest evidence would come from randomly assigning firms to the shorter schedule, an experiment few governments have been willing to fund.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["By contrast,","Similarly,","Instead,","Either way,"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The sentence states a conclusion that holds whichever side is right, since random assignment would settle the dispute. “Either way” expresses that.","why":["“By contrast” implies the sentence opposes the critics, but it applies to both sides.","“Similarly” signals a parallel point, not a conclusion covering both views.","“Instead” would present the experiment as a replacement for something just mentioned.","Correct. Whichever side is right, random assignment gives the strongest evidence."]},{"n":23,"domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":50,"intl":"“On the contrary” vs. “By contrast”","passage":"<p>Residents of the northern district are mostly employed in fishing. ______ residents of the southern district work mainly in tourism.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Consequently,","By contrast,","On the contrary,","Accordingly,"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The two sentences compare two groups that differ. “By contrast” marks the difference. “On the contrary” is used to reject a claim that was just made, which does not happen here.","why":["“Consequently” signals a result, but southern employment is not caused by northern employment.","Correct. “By contrast” introduces a comparison between two different cases.","“On the contrary” denies a previous statement. Nothing is being denied, so it is a frequent learner error here.","“Accordingly” signals a result or consequence, which is not the relationship."]},{"n":24,"domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":75,"passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Andean Seed Institute compared two methods of storing seed.</li><li>Seed stored in sealed clay jars retained 91 percent germination after ten years.</li><li>Seed stored in paper bags retained 34 percent germination after ten years.</li><li>Storage in jars cost about three times as much per kilogram of seed as storage in bags.</li></ul>","prompt":"The student wants to emphasize the trade-off between cost and seed viability. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["After ten years, seed stored in sealed clay jars retained 91 percent germination, compared with 34 percent for seed stored in paper bags.","Because paper bags were cheaper, they were the better choice for storing seed for ten years.","Although storage in sealed clay jars cost about three times as much per kilogram of seed as storage in paper bags, seed kept in jars retained 91 percent germination after ten years, compared with 34 percent for seed kept in bags.","Sealed clay jars kept seed viable for three times as long as paper bags did."],"answer":2,"explanation":"A trade-off requires both sides: the higher cost and the better viability. Only choice C states both accurately.","why":["Gives only the viability results and omits cost, so no trade-off appears.","Unsupported. The notes do not say bags were better, and their viability was much lower.","Correct. States the higher cost of jars and the higher germination they achieved.","Misuses the cost ratio as a duration. The notes do not say jars kept seed viable three times as long."]},{"n":25,"domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":75,"passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li><i>Salt and Iron</i> (2018) is a novel by the Ghanaian writer Abena Osei.</li><li>The novel follows three generations of a family of salt traders.</li><li>It won the 2019 Halloran Prize for fiction.</li><li>Critics have praised its use of shifting narrators.</li></ul>","prompt":"The student wants to introduce the novel to readers who are unfamiliar with it. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["<i>Salt and Iron</i> (2018), a novel by the Ghanaian writer Abena Osei, follows three generations of a family of salt traders and won the 2019 Halloran Prize for fiction.","Critics have praised Abena Osei’s use of shifting narrators.","Praised for its shifting narrators, Abena Osei’s <i>Salt and Iron</i> is widely regarded as the finest African novel of its decade.","The Halloran Prize, awarded in 2019, is among the most prestigious literary awards in the world."],"answer":0,"explanation":"An introduction should identify the work, its author, and what it is about, and may add a mark of recognition. Choice A does this and stays within the notes.","why":["Correct. Gives the title, date, author, subject, and a prize, all from the notes.","Mentions praise only and does not identify the novel or what it is about.","“Widely regarded as the finest African novel of its decade” is not in the notes.","The prize’s prestige is not in the notes, and the sentence does not mention the novel."]},{"n":26,"domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":85,"passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>A study tracked 5,200 pupils in 40 schools taking part in a national free school-meal programme.</li><li>Attendance rose by an average of 2.1 percentage points in participating schools.</li><li>Test scores showed no statistically significant change.</li><li>The study lasted one academic year.</li></ul>","prompt":"The student wants to summarize the study’s findings while noting a limit on what the study can show. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The free school-meal programme improved pupils’ learning by raising attendance by 2.1 percent.","In a study of 5,200 pupils, a free school-meal programme raised attendance by an average of 2.1 percentage points but left test scores unchanged.","In a one-year study of 5,200 pupils, a free school-meal programme raised attendance by an average of 2.1 percentage points but produced no statistically significant change in test scores, so longer-term effects remain unknown.","Attendance rose by 2.1 percent in 40 schools, and test scores were unaffected."],"answer":2,"explanation":"The goal requires the findings (attendance up, scores unchanged) and a limit on the study. The one-year duration is that limit, and choice C uses it while correctly saying “percentage points.”","why":["Claims improved learning, which the test scores contradict, and confuses 2.1 percentage points with 2.1 percent.","States the findings but gives no limit, and “left test scores unchanged” overstates “no statistically significant change.”","Correct. Accurate findings, correct unit, and the one-year duration as a limit.","Uses “percent” instead of “percentage points,” omits the limit, and overstates “unaffected.”"]},{"n":27,"domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":85,"passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>In 2022, researchers reported a new sodium-based battery electrode.</li><li>The electrode retained 90 percent of its capacity after 5,000 charge cycles.</li><li>Sodium is far more abundant and widely distributed than lithium.</li><li>The prototype has been tested only in small laboratory cells.</li></ul>","prompt":"The student wants to explain why the discovery is promising while acknowledging that it is preliminary. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The new sodium-based electrode will soon replace lithium in batteries because sodium is more abundant.","A 2022 sodium-based electrode that retained 90 percent of its capacity after 5,000 charge cycles is promising because sodium is far more abundant than lithium, although the prototype has so far been tested only in small laboratory cells.","Researchers reported in 2022 that a sodium-based electrode retained 90 percent of its capacity after 5,000 charge cycles.","Because the prototype has been tested only in small laboratory cells, sodium-based electrodes are unlikely to be useful."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The goal has two parts: why it is promising (durability and abundant sodium) and why it is preliminary (small laboratory cells). Only choice B provides both without overstating.","why":["“Will soon replace” is not supported and ignores that the work is preliminary.","Correct. Gives the durability result, the abundance of sodium, and the limitation of small-scale testing.","States the result only and gives no reason it is promising beyond the figure, and no acknowledgment that it is preliminary.","Overreaches: limited testing does not show that the electrodes are unlikely to be useful."]}]},"m1":{"id":"m1","section":"Math","module":1,"title":"Math · Module 1","minutes":35,"rich":true,"questions":[{"n":1,"domain":"Algebra","skill":"Linear functions","difficulty":"EASY","targetSec":50,"prompt":"The graph of <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = −3<i>x</i> + 7 is translated 4 units to the left and 2 units down to obtain the graph of <i>g</i>. Which equation defines <i>g</i>?","choices":["<i>g</i>(<i>x</i>) = −3<i>x</i> + 17","<i>g</i>(<i>x</i>) = −3<i>x</i> − 7","<i>g</i>(<i>x</i>) = −3<i>x</i> − 5","<i>g</i>(<i>x</i>) = −3<i>x</i> + 5"],"answer":1,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>A shift 4 units left replaces <i>x</i> with <i>x</i> + 4, and a shift 2 units down subtracts 2: <i>g</i>(<i>x</i>) = <i>f</i>(<i>x</i> + 4) − 2.</li><li><i>f</i>(<i>x</i> + 4) = −3(<i>x</i> + 4) + 7 = −3<i>x</i> − 5.</li><li>Subtract 2: <i>g</i>(<i>x</i>) = −3<i>x</i> − 7.</li></ol>","why":["This is <i>f</i>(<i>x</i> − 4) − 2, a shift 4 units to the <b>right</b>. Left means adding 4 inside the function.","Correct. <i>g</i>(<i>x</i>) = <i>f</i>(<i>x</i> + 4) − 2 = −3<i>x</i> − 7.","This applies only the horizontal shift, <i>f</i>(<i>x</i> + 4), and forgets the 2-unit downward shift.","This applies only the vertical shift, <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) − 2, and forgets the horizontal shift."]},{"n":2,"type":"grid","domain":"Algebra","skill":"Linear functions (modelling)","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":75,"prompt":"The cost <i>C</i>(<i>n</i>), in euros, of manufacturing <i>n</i> identical circuit boards is a linear function of <i>n</i>. Manufacturing 40 boards costs 1,150 euros, and manufacturing 90 boards costs 1,900 euros. A client has a budget of 4,000 euros. What is the greatest number of boards that can be manufactured within the budget?","nums":[230],"display":"230","explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Slope (cost per board) = (1,900 − 1,150) ÷ (90 − 40) = 750 ÷ 50 = 15 euros.</li><li><i>C</i>(<i>n</i>) = 15<i>n</i> + <i>b</i>. Using 40 boards: 1,150 = 15(40) + <i>b</i>, so <i>b</i> = 550.</li><li>Budget condition: 15<i>n</i> + 550 ≤ 4,000, so 15<i>n</i> ≤ 3,450 and <i>n</i> ≤ 230.</li><li>The greatest whole number of boards is <b>230</b>.</li></ol>"},{"n":3,"domain":"Algebra","skill":"Linear equations in one variable","difficulty":"EASY","targetSec":75,"prompt":"What value of <i>x</i> satisfies the equation <span class=\"frac\"><span>3<i>x</i> − 1</span><span>4</span></span> − <span class=\"frac\"><span><i>x</i> + 2</span><span>6</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>5</span><span>12</span></span> ?","choices":["<span class=\"frac\"><span>12</span><span>7</span></span>","<span class=\"frac\"><span>4</span><span>11</span></span>","<span class=\"frac\"><span>6</span><span>7</span></span>","<span class=\"frac\"><span>7</span><span>12</span></span>"],"answer":0,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Multiply every term by the common denominator 12: 3(3<i>x</i> − 1) − 2(<i>x</i> + 2) = 5.</li><li>Distribute carefully: 9<i>x</i> − 3 − 2<i>x</i> − 4 = 5.</li><li>Combine: 7<i>x</i> − 7 = 5, so 7<i>x</i> = 12.</li><li><i>x</i> = 12/7.</li></ol>","why":["Correct. 9<i>x</i> − 3 − 2<i>x</i> − 4 = 5 gives 7<i>x</i> = 12.","This comes from a sign error on the second fraction: 9<i>x</i> − 3 + 2<i>x</i> + 4 = 5 gives 11<i>x</i> = 4.","This comes from not distributing the minus sign onto the 2: 9<i>x</i> − 3 − 2<i>x</i> + 2 = 5 gives 7<i>x</i> = 6.","This is the reciprocal of the correct value. Substituting it into the left side does not give 5/12."]},{"n":4,"domain":"Algebra","skill":"Linear inequalities in two variables","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":85,"prompt":"A cyclist rides <i>x</i> kilometres on flat road at an average speed of 24 km/h and <i>y</i> kilometres on hills at an average speed of 15 km/h. The cyclist wants to ride a total of at least 60 kilometres in at most 3 hours, with at least 20 kilometres on flat road. Which ordered pair (<i>x</i>, <i>y</i>) satisfies all of these conditions?","choices":["(40, 20)","(20, 40)","(24, 36)","(30, 30)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>The conditions are <i>x</i> + <i>y</i> ≥ 60, <i>x</i> ≥ 20, and the time condition <i>x</i>/24 + <i>y</i>/15 ≤ 3.</li><li>All four pairs have <i>x</i> + <i>y</i> = 60 and <i>x</i> ≥ 20, so time decides.</li><li>(40, 20): 40/24 + 20/15 = 1.667 + 1.333 = 3.0 ≤ 3. ✓</li><li>(20, 40): 0.833 + 2.667 = 3.5. ✗ (24, 36): 1 + 2.4 = 3.4. ✗ (30, 30): 1.25 + 2 = 3.25. ✗</li></ol>","why":["Correct. The total time is exactly 3.0 hours.","Total time is 20/24 + 40/15 ≈ 3.5 hours, which is more than 3.","Total time is 24/24 + 36/15 = 3.4 hours, which is more than 3.","Total time is 30/24 + 30/15 = 3.25 hours, which is more than 3."]},{"n":5,"type":"grid","domain":"Algebra","skill":"Absolute value equations","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":100,"prompt":"What is the sum of all values of <i>x</i> that satisfy the equation |2<i>x</i> − 7| = 3|<i>x</i> + 1| ?","nums":[-9.2],"display":"−9.2 (or −46/5)","explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Two absolute values are equal when the expressions inside are equal or opposite. Case 1: 2<i>x</i> − 7 = 3(<i>x</i> + 1) gives 2<i>x</i> − 7 = 3<i>x</i> + 3, so <i>x</i> = −10.</li><li>Case 2: 2<i>x</i> − 7 = −3(<i>x</i> + 1) gives 2<i>x</i> − 7 = −3<i>x</i> − 3, so 5<i>x</i> = 4 and <i>x</i> = 0.8.</li><li>Check: <i>x</i> = −10 gives |−27| = 27 and 3|−9| = 27. ✓ <i>x</i> = 0.8 gives |−5.4| = 5.4 and 3|1.8| = 5.4. ✓</li><li>Sum = −10 + 0.8 = <b>−9.2</b>.</li></ol>","desmosTip":"Graph <i>y</i> = abs(2<i>x</i> − 7) and <i>y</i> = 3abs(<i>x</i> + 1). The two intersection points have <i>x</i>-values −10 and 0.8."},{"n":6,"type":"grid","domain":"Algebra","skill":"Linear equations in two variables","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":85,"prompt":"In the <i>xy</i>-plane, line ℓ passes through the point (2, −3) and is perpendicular to the line with equation 5<i>x</i> − 2<i>y</i> = 9. Line ℓ crosses the <i>y</i>-axis at the point (0, <i>b</i>). What is the value of <i>b</i>?","nums":[-2.2],"display":"−2.2 (or −11/5)","explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Rewrite 5<i>x</i> − 2<i>y</i> = 9 as <i>y</i> = (5/2)<i>x</i> − 9/2, so its slope is 5/2.</li><li>A perpendicular line has slope −2/5.</li><li>ℓ: <i>y</i> = −(2/5)<i>x</i> + <i>b</i>. Substitute (2, −3): −3 = −4/5 + <i>b</i>.</li><li><i>b</i> = −3 + 4/5 = −11/5 = <b>−2.2</b>.</li></ol>"},{"n":7,"domain":"Algebra","skill":"Systems of linear inequalities (optimisation)","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":130,"prompt":"A workshop makes chairs and tables each week. Each chair needs 2 hours of labour and 3 kilograms of timber. Each table needs 5 hours of labour and 4 kilograms of timber. The workshop has at most 100 hours of labour and 108 kilograms of timber available each week. A chair earns a profit of 30 euros and a table earns a profit of 70 euros. If the workshop makes whole numbers of chairs and tables, what is the greatest possible weekly profit, in euros?","choices":["1,080","1,400","1,440","1,500"],"answer":2,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Let <i>x</i> = chairs and <i>y</i> = tables. Constraints: 2<i>x</i> + 5<i>y</i> ≤ 100 (labour), 3<i>x</i> + 4<i>y</i> ≤ 108 (timber), <i>x</i>, <i>y</i> ≥ 0. Maximise 30<i>x</i> + 70<i>y</i>.</li><li>The maximum occurs at a corner of the feasible region. Corners: (0, 0), (36, 0), (0, 20), and the intersection of the two constraint lines.</li><li>Intersection: 2<i>x</i> + 5<i>y</i> = 100 and 3<i>x</i> + 4<i>y</i> = 108. Multiply the first by 3 and the second by 2: 6<i>x</i> + 15<i>y</i> = 300 and 6<i>x</i> + 8<i>y</i> = 216, so 7<i>y</i> = 84 and <i>y</i> = 12, <i>x</i> = 20. These are whole numbers.</li><li>Profits: (36, 0) → 1,080; (0, 20) → 1,400; (20, 12) → 600 + 840 = <b>1,440</b>.</li></ol>","why":["1,080 is the profit from making only chairs (36 chairs), which leaves labour unused.","1,400 is the profit from making only tables (20 tables). It is feasible but not the maximum.","Correct. Making 20 chairs and 12 tables uses exactly 100 hours and 108 kg and earns 1,440 euros.","1,500 would need 50 chairs (100 hours of labour), but 50 chairs need 150 kg of timber, which exceeds 108 kg."]},{"n":8,"domain":"Algebra","skill":"Systems of linear equations","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":130,"passage":"<p style=\"font-size:20px\"><i>kx</i> + 8<i>y</i> = 20<br>3<i>x</i> + (<i>k</i> − 10)<i>y</i> = −30</p>","prompt":"For which value of <i>k</i> does the system of equations above have no solution?","choices":["−2","4","10","12"],"answer":3,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>A system of two lines has no solution when the lines are parallel but different. First find where the slopes match: <i>k</i>/3 = 8/(<i>k</i> − 10).</li><li>Cross-multiply: <i>k</i>(<i>k</i> − 10) = 24, so <i>k</i><sup>2</sup> − 10<i>k</i> − 24 = 0 and (<i>k</i> − 12)(<i>k</i> + 2) = 0. So <i>k</i> = 12 or <i>k</i> = −2.</li><li>Test <i>k</i> = −2: the equations are −2<i>x</i> + 8<i>y</i> = 20 and 3<i>x</i> − 12<i>y</i> = −30. Multiply the first by −1.5: 3<i>x</i> − 12<i>y</i> = −30. Same line, so infinitely many solutions.</li><li>Test <i>k</i> = 12: 12<i>x</i> + 8<i>y</i> = 20 and 3<i>x</i> + 2<i>y</i> = −30. Multiply the second by 4: 12<i>x</i> + 8<i>y</i> = −120, which contradicts 20. Parallel, so no solution.</li></ol>","why":["<i>k</i> = −2 makes the lines parallel, but they are the <b>same</b> line, so there are infinitely many solutions, not none.","For <i>k</i> = 4 the slopes are different (−4/8 and −3/(−6)), so the lines cross at exactly one point.","For <i>k</i> = 10 the second equation becomes 3<i>x</i> = −30, so <i>x</i> = −10 and <i>y</i> = 15. There is exactly one solution.","Correct. Parallel and distinct lines, so there is no solution."]},{"n":9,"type":"grid","domain":"Algebra","skill":"Nonlinear systems and structure","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":130,"prompt":"The sum of two positive numbers <i>x</i> and <i>y</i> is 16, and <span class=\"frac\"><span><i>x</i></span><span><i>y</i></span></span> + <span class=\"frac\"><span><i>y</i></span><span><i>x</i></span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>34</span><span>15</span></span>. What is the value of <i>xy</i>?","nums":[60],"display":"60","explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Combine the fractions: <i>x</i>/<i>y</i> + <i>y</i>/<i>x</i> = (<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>y</i><sup>2</sup>)/(<i>xy</i>).</li><li>Use (<i>x</i> + <i>y</i>)<sup>2</sup> = <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>y</i><sup>2</sup> + 2<i>xy</i>, so <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>y</i><sup>2</sup> = 256 − 2<i>xy</i>.</li><li>Let <i>p</i> = <i>xy</i>: (256 − 2<i>p</i>)/<i>p</i> = 34/15.</li><li>15(256 − 2<i>p</i>) = 34<i>p</i>, so 3,840 − 30<i>p</i> = 34<i>p</i>, so 64<i>p</i> = 3,840 and <i>p</i> = <b>60</b>.</li><li>Check: <i>x</i> = 6 and <i>y</i> = 10 give sum 16, product 60, and 6/10 + 10/6 = 34/15. ✓</li></ol>"},{"n":10,"domain":"Advanced Math","skill":"Exponential functions","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":85,"prompt":"A bacterial culture’s population is modelled by <i>P</i>(<i>t</i>) = 500(1.6)<sup><i>t</i>/4</sup>, where <i>t</i> is the number of hours since the first measurement. According to the model, after how many hours will the population first be double its initial value, to the nearest tenth of an hour?","choices":["4.0","5.9","6.4","8.0"],"answer":1,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Doubling means <i>P</i>(<i>t</i>) = 1,000, so 500(1.6)<sup><i>t</i>/4</sup> = 1,000 and (1.6)<sup><i>t</i>/4</sup> = 2.</li><li>Take logarithms: (<i>t</i>/4) ln 1.6 = ln 2, so <i>t</i> = 4 ln 2 ÷ ln 1.6.</li><li><i>t</i> = 4 × 0.6931 ÷ 0.4700 ≈ 5.899, which rounds to <b>5.9</b> hours.</li></ol>","desmosTip":"Graph <i>y</i> = 500(1.6)^(<i>x</i>/4) and <i>y</i> = 1000. Click the intersection to read <i>x</i> ≈ 5.899.","why":["At <i>t</i> = 4 the population is 500 × 1.6 = 800, which is a 60% increase, not a doubling.","Correct. (1.6)<sup>5.899/4</sup> = 2.","6.4 is 4 × 1.6. At <i>t</i> = 6.4 the population is about 1,061, not 1,000.","At <i>t</i> = 8 the population is 500(1.6)<sup>2</sup> = 1,280, which is more than double."]},{"n":11,"domain":"Advanced Math","skill":"Polynomial remainder theorem","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":85,"prompt":"When <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = 2<i>x</i><sup>3</sup> + <i>ax</i><sup>2</sup> − 7<i>x</i> + 6 is divided by <i>x</i> + 2, the remainder is −8. What is the value of <i>a</i>?","choices":["−4","−3","−2","1"],"answer":1,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>By the remainder theorem, the remainder when dividing by <i>x</i> + 2 is <i>f</i>(−2).</li><li><i>f</i>(−2) = 2(−8) + <i>a</i>(4) − 7(−2) + 6 = −16 + 4<i>a</i> + 14 + 6 = 4<i>a</i> + 4.</li><li>Set 4<i>a</i> + 4 = −8, so 4<i>a</i> = −12 and <i>a</i> = <b>−3</b>.</li></ol>","why":["This comes from evaluating <i>f</i>(2), which is the remainder for division by <i>x</i> − 2. For <i>x</i> + 2 you must use −2.","Correct. <i>f</i>(−2) = 4(−3) + 4 = −8.","With <i>a</i> = −2, <i>f</i>(−2) = 4(−2) + 4 = −4, not −8.","With <i>a</i> = 1, <i>f</i>(−2) = 4(1) + 4 = 8, not −8."]},{"n":12,"domain":"Advanced Math","skill":"Equivalent rational expressions","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":85,"prompt":"For <i>x</i> &gt; 4, which expression is equivalent to <span class=\"frac\"><span><i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 16</span><span><i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 3<i>x</i> − 4</span></span> ÷ <span class=\"frac\"><span><i>x</i> + 4</span><span><i>x</i> + 1</span></span> ?","choices":["<i>x</i> − 4","<span class=\"frac\"><span><i>x</i> − 4</span><span><i>x</i> + 1</span></span>","<span class=\"frac\"><span><i>x</i> + 1</span><span><i>x</i> + 4</span></span>","1"],"answer":3,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Factor: <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 16 = (<i>x</i> − 4)(<i>x</i> + 4) and <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 3<i>x</i> − 4 = (<i>x</i> − 4)(<i>x</i> + 1).</li><li>The first fraction simplifies to (<i>x</i> + 4)/(<i>x</i> + 1), since the factor (<i>x</i> − 4) cancels (allowed because <i>x</i> &gt; 4).</li><li>Dividing by (<i>x</i> + 4)/(<i>x</i> + 1) means multiplying by its reciprocal: (<i>x</i> + 4)/(<i>x</i> + 1) × (<i>x</i> + 1)/(<i>x</i> + 4) = <b>1</b>.</li><li>Check with <i>x</i> = 6: (20/14) ÷ (10/7) = (10/7) ÷ (10/7) = 1. ✓</li></ol>","why":["At <i>x</i> = 6 the original expression equals 1, but this choice equals 2.","At <i>x</i> = 6 this choice equals 2/7, not 1.","At <i>x</i> = 6 this choice equals 7/10, not 1. It is what remains if the reciprocal is applied to only part of the expression.","Correct. After simplifying and using the reciprocal, everything cancels."]},{"n":13,"domain":"Advanced Math","skill":"Quadratic functions and inequalities","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":85,"prompt":"Let <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = −(<i>x</i> + 1)<sup>2</sup> + 9. For how many integer values of <i>x</i> is <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) &gt; 0?","choices":["3","4","5","6"],"answer":2,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li><i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) &gt; 0 means (<i>x</i> + 1)<sup>2</sup> &lt; 9, so −3 &lt; <i>x</i> + 1 &lt; 3.</li><li>Subtract 1: −4 &lt; <i>x</i> &lt; 2.</li><li>The integers strictly between −4 and 2 are −3, −2, −1, 0, 1, which is <b>5</b> values.</li></ol>","desmosTip":"Graph <i>y</i> = −(<i>x</i> + 1)^2 + 9 and count the integer <i>x</i>-values where the curve is above the <i>x</i>-axis.","why":["This undercounts. It misses two of the integers between −4 and 2.","This misses one of the five integers (−3, −2, −1, 0, 1).","Correct. −3, −2, −1, 0, 1.","This wrongly counts an endpoint. At <i>x</i> = −4 and at <i>x</i> = 2, <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = 0, which is not greater than 0."]},{"n":14,"domain":"Advanced Math","skill":"Radical equations","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":100,"prompt":"What is the sum of all real solutions of the equation <span class=\"sqrt\">√<span>3<i>x</i> + 7</span></span> − <i>x</i> = 1 ?","choices":["−2","1","3","6"],"answer":2,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Isolate the radical: √(3<i>x</i> + 7) = <i>x</i> + 1.</li><li>Square both sides: 3<i>x</i> + 7 = <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + 2<i>x</i> + 1, so <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − <i>x</i> − 6 = 0 and (<i>x</i> − 3)(<i>x</i> + 2) = 0.</li><li>Check both candidates in the original equation. <i>x</i> = 3: √16 − 3 = 1. ✓ <i>x</i> = −2: √1 − (−2) = 3 ≠ 1. ✗ (the radical must equal <i>x</i> + 1 = −1, which is impossible).</li><li>Only <i>x</i> = 3 is a solution, so the sum is <b>3</b>.</li></ol>","desmosTip":"Graph <i>y</i> = sqrt(3<i>x</i> + 7) − <i>x</i> and <i>y</i> = 1. They intersect only at <i>x</i> = 3, showing that −2 is extraneous.","why":["−2 is an extraneous solution created by squaring. It does not satisfy the original equation.","1 is the sum of both roots of the squared equation (3 + (−2)). It includes the extraneous root.","Correct. Only <i>x</i> = 3 works, so the sum of all solutions is 3.","Nothing in the work produces 6. It would double the valid solution."]},{"n":15,"type":"grid","domain":"Advanced Math","skill":"Systems of a line and a parabola","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":110,"prompt":"In the <i>xy</i>-plane, the graph of <i>y</i> = 2<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 5<i>x</i> + 3 and the graph of <i>y</i> = 3<i>x</i> + <i>c</i>, where <i>c</i> is a constant, intersect at exactly one point. What is the value of <i>c</i>?","nums":[-5],"display":"−5","explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Set the expressions equal: 2<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 5<i>x</i> + 3 = 3<i>x</i> + <i>c</i>, so 2<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 8<i>x</i> + (3 − <i>c</i>) = 0.</li><li>Exactly one intersection means the discriminant is 0: (−8)<sup>2</sup> − 4(2)(3 − <i>c</i>) = 0.</li><li>64 − 24 + 8<i>c</i> = 0, so 8<i>c</i> = −40 and <i>c</i> = <b>−5</b>.</li><li>Check: 2<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 8<i>x</i> + 8 = 2(<i>x</i> − 2)<sup>2</sup> = 0, so the single intersection is at <i>x</i> = 2. ✓</li></ol>","desmosTip":"Graph <i>y</i> = 2<i>x</i>^2 − 5<i>x</i> + 3 and <i>y</i> = 3<i>x</i> + <i>c</i>, then add a slider for <i>c</i>. The line just touches the parabola at <i>c</i> = −5."},{"n":16,"domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill":"Statistics: mean and outliers","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":85,"prompt":"A data set of 8 values is 12, 15, 15, 18, 20, 22, 25, and <i>x</i>. The mean of the data set is 20. If <i>x</i> is replaced with 45, by how much does the mean of the data set increase?","choices":["1.5","3","12","21.5"],"answer":0,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>The seven known values sum to 12 + 15 + 15 + 18 + 20 + 22 + 25 = 127. A mean of 20 for 8 values means the total is 160, so <i>x</i> = 33.</li><li>Replacing 33 with 45 increases the total by 12, from 160 to 172.</li><li>The new mean is 172 ÷ 8 = 21.5. The increase is 21.5 − 20 = <b>1.5</b>. (Equivalently, 12 ÷ 8 = 1.5.)</li></ol>","why":["Correct. The total rises by 12 and it is spread over 8 values.","3 comes from dividing the change of 12 by 4 instead of 8.","12 is the change in the <i>total</i>, not in the mean.","21.5 is the new mean, not the increase."]},{"n":17,"domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill":"Two-way tables and conditional probability","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":85,"passage":"<p>In a survey of 400 commuters, each person gave their main mode of travel to work. The table shows the results by age group.</p>","table":{"caption":"Main mode of travel to work, by age group","headers":["Age group","Cycle","Bus","Car","Total"],"rows":[["Under 30","64","52","24","140"],["30–49","36","44","60","140"],["50 and over","20","34","66","120"],["Total","120","130","150","400"]]},"prompt":"One participant is selected at random from those who do <b>not</b> travel to work by car. What is the probability that the participant is under 30?","choices":["<span class=\"frac\"><span>116</span><span>250</span></span>","<span class=\"frac\"><span>116</span><span>400</span></span>","<span class=\"frac\"><span>116</span><span>140</span></span>","<span class=\"frac\"><span>140</span><span>250</span></span>"],"answer":0,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Those who do not use a car: 120 (cycle) + 130 (bus) = 250. This is the new sample space.</li><li>Of these, the under-30 participants: 64 + 52 = 116.</li><li>Probability = 116/250 = 58/125 = 0.464.</li></ol>","why":["Correct. 116 of the 250 non-car commuters are under 30.","This divides by all 400 commuters, ignoring the condition that the person does not travel by car.","This is the probability of not using a car <i>given</i> under 30, with the condition reversed.","This uses all 140 under-30 commuters, including the 24 who use a car, as the numerator."]},{"n":18,"domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill":"Inference and margin of error","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":75,"prompt":"A researcher surveyed a random sample of 240 residents of a city with 90,000 residents. In the sample, 35% owned a bicycle. The margin of error for this estimate was 6 percentage points at a 95% confidence level. Which conclusion is best supported by these results?","choices":["Exactly 35% of the city’s residents own a bicycle.","Between 29% and 41% of the 240 sampled residents own a bicycle.","The survey proves that at least 95% of the city’s residents own a bicycle within 6% of the sample result.","It is plausible that between 29% and 41% of all the city’s residents own a bicycle."],"answer":3,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>The margin of error describes how far the <i>population</i> percentage could plausibly be from the sample percentage.</li><li>35% ± 6 percentage points gives the interval 29% to 41%.</li><li>The interval applies to the whole city, not to the sample, and it expresses plausibility, not certainty.</li></ol>","why":["A sample estimate is not an exact population value. The margin of error shows the uncertainty.","The sample itself is known exactly: 35% of the 240 sampled residents. The interval is for the city, not the sample.","This garbles the meaning of the confidence level and claims a proof, but statistics cannot prove this.","Correct. The interval 29% to 41% describes plausible values for the whole city."]},{"n":19,"type":"grid","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill":"Linear models and residuals","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":100,"prompt":"A shop owner models daily sales <i>y</i> (units) against midday temperature <i>x</i> (°C) with the line of best fit <i>ŷ</i> = 12.5<i>x</i> − 110. On a day when the temperature was 26 °C, the shop actually sold 231 units. The model’s prediction fell short of the actual sales. By what percent of the actual sales did it fall short? Round your answer to the nearest tenth of a percent.","nums":[6.9],"tol":0.001,"display":"6.9","explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Predicted sales at 26 °C: 12.5(26) − 110 = 325 − 110 = 215 units.</li><li>Shortfall: 231 − 215 = 16 units.</li><li>As a percent of the <b>actual</b> sales: 16 ÷ 231 ≈ 0.0693 = 6.93%, which rounds to <b>6.9</b>.</li></ol>","desmosTip":"Type 16/231*100 directly into an expression line to read 6.926…."},{"n":20,"domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill":"Circles in the coordinate plane","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":85,"prompt":"The equation of a circle in the <i>xy</i>-plane is <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>y</i><sup>2</sup> − 10<i>x</i> + 4<i>y</i> + 13 = 0. What is the greatest <i>y</i>-coordinate of any point on the circle?","choices":["−6","−2","2","4"],"answer":2,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Complete the square: (<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 10<i>x</i> + 25) + (<i>y</i><sup>2</sup> + 4<i>y</i> + 4) = −13 + 25 + 4.</li><li>(<i>x</i> − 5)<sup>2</sup> + (<i>y</i> + 2)<sup>2</sup> = 16. The centre is (5, −2) and the radius is 4.</li><li>The highest point is directly above the centre: <i>y</i> = −2 + 4 = <b>2</b>.</li></ol>","why":["−6 is the <i>lowest</i> point of the circle, −2 − 4.","−2 is the <i>y</i>-coordinate of the centre, not the highest point.","Correct. Centre <i>y</i> plus the radius: −2 + 4 = 2.","4 is the radius. The highest <i>y</i> is the centre’s <i>y</i> plus the radius."]},{"n":21,"domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill":"Right triangle trigonometry","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":95,"passage":"<svg class=\"fig\" viewBox=\"0 0 220 215\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Right triangle ABC with right angle at C. Point D lies on the hypotenuse AB and CD is perpendicular to AB.\"><g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"><path d=\"M30 190 L30 30 L150 30 Z\"/><path d=\"M30 30 L106.8 87.6\"/><path d=\"M42 30 V42 H30\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path d=\"M112.8 79.6 L104.8 73.6 L98.8 81.6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/></g><text x=\"10\" y=\"202\">A</text><text x=\"156\" y=\"36\">B</text><text x=\"12\" y=\"26\">C</text><text x=\"112\" y=\"104\">D</text></svg>","prompt":"In the figure, triangle <i>ABC</i> has a right angle at <i>C</i>, tan <i>A</i> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>3</span><span>4</span></span>, and <i>AB</i> = 25 metres. Point <i>D</i> lies on <i>AB</i> so that <i>CD</i> is perpendicular to <i>AB</i>. What is the length, in metres, of <i>CD</i>?","choices":["9","12","15","16"],"answer":1,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>tan <i>A</i> = <i>BC</i>/<i>AC</i> = 3/4, so the triangle is similar to a 3-4-5 triangle. With hypotenuse 25 (five times 5), <i>BC</i> = 15 and <i>AC</i> = 20.</li><li>Area of the triangle = ½ × <i>AC</i> × <i>BC</i> = ½ × 20 × 15 = 150 m<sup>2</sup>.</li><li>The same area equals ½ × <i>AB</i> × <i>CD</i> = ½ × 25 × <i>CD</i>. So 12.5 <i>CD</i> = 150 and <i>CD</i> = <b>12</b>.</li></ol>","why":["9 is the length of <i>BD</i> (= <i>BC</i><sup>2</sup>/<i>AB</i> = 225/25), not of <i>CD</i>.","Correct. <i>CD</i> = (<i>AC</i> × <i>BC</i>)/<i>AB</i> = 300/25 = 12.","15 is the length of side <i>BC</i>, not the altitude to the hypotenuse.","16 is the length of <i>AD</i> (= <i>AC</i><sup>2</sup>/<i>AB</i> = 400/25), not of <i>CD</i>."]},{"n":22,"domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill":"Volume of solids","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":100,"prompt":"A solid metal sphere with radius 6 centimetres is melted and recast, with no loss of metal, into a solid right circular cone with base radius 4 centimetres. What is the height, in centimetres, of the cone?","choices":["13.5","18","27","54"],"answer":3,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Volume of the sphere = <sup>4</sup>⁄<sub>3</sub>π(6)<sup>3</sup> = <sup>4</sup>⁄<sub>3</sub>π(216) = 288π cm<sup>3</sup>.</li><li>Volume of the cone = <sup>1</sup>⁄<sub>3</sub>π(4)<sup>2</sup><i>h</i> = <sup>16</sup>⁄<sub>3</sub>π<i>h</i>.</li><li>Set them equal: <sup>16</sup>⁄<sub>3</sub><i>h</i> = 288, so <i>h</i> = 288 × 3 ÷ 16 = <b>54</b> cm.</li></ol>","why":["13.5 comes from using the base <i>diameter</i> (8) as the radius of the cone.","18 comes from forgetting the factor <sup>1</sup>⁄<sub>3</sub> in the cone’s volume formula: 288π = 16π<i>h</i>.","27 comes from using the sphere’s surface area 4π<i>r</i><sup>2</sup> = 144π instead of its volume.","Correct. Equal volumes give <i>h</i> = 54 cm."]}]},"m2":{"id":"m2","section":"Math","module":2,"title":"Math · Module 2","minutes":35,"rich":true,"questions":[{"n":1,"domain":"Algebra","skill":"Linear functions (modelling)","difficulty":"EASY","targetSec":55,"prompt":"A survey drone descends at a constant rate. At <i>t</i> = 12 seconds its altitude is 90 metres, and at <i>t</i> = 20 seconds its altitude is 62 metres. Which equation gives the drone’s altitude <i>a</i>, in metres, <i>t</i> seconds after the start?","choices":["<i>a</i> = −3.5<i>t</i> + 90","<i>a</i> = −28<i>t</i> + 426","<i>a</i> = −3.5<i>t</i> + 62","<i>a</i> = −3.5<i>t</i> + 132"],"answer":3,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Rate of change = (62 − 90) ÷ (20 − 12) = −28 ÷ 8 = −3.5 metres per second.</li><li>Use <i>a</i> = −3.5<i>t</i> + <i>b</i> with the point (12, 90): 90 = −3.5(12) + <i>b</i> = −42 + <i>b</i>, so <i>b</i> = 132.</li><li><i>a</i> = −3.5<i>t</i> + 132. Check at <i>t</i> = 20: −70 + 132 = 62. ✓</li></ol>","why":["This uses the altitude at 12 seconds as the starting altitude. At <i>t</i> = 0 the altitude is 132 metres, not 90.","This uses the total change in altitude (−28 m) as the rate. The rate must be divided by the 8 seconds elapsed.","This uses the altitude at 20 seconds as the starting altitude, which is not the altitude at <i>t</i> = 0.","Correct. The slope is −3.5 and the intercept is 132."]},{"n":2,"domain":"Algebra","skill":"Compound inequalities and absolute value","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":80,"prompt":"How many integers <i>x</i> satisfy both |<i>x</i> − 4| ≤ 3 and 2<i>x</i> + 1 &gt; 5 ?","choices":["4","5","6","7"],"answer":1,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>|<i>x</i> − 4| ≤ 3 means −3 ≤ <i>x</i> − 4 ≤ 3, so 1 ≤ <i>x</i> ≤ 7.</li><li>2<i>x</i> + 1 &gt; 5 means 2<i>x</i> &gt; 4, so <i>x</i> &gt; 2.</li><li>Both hold when 2 &lt; <i>x</i> ≤ 7. The integers are 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, which is <b>5</b> integers.</li></ol>","why":["This drops one of the integers 3 to 7. Note that <i>x</i> = 7 is included because the first inequality is “≤”.","Correct. 3, 4, 5, 6, 7.","This includes <i>x</i> = 2, but 2(2) + 1 = 5 is not greater than 5.","This counts all integers from 1 to 7 and ignores the second inequality."]},{"n":3,"type":"grid","domain":"Algebra","skill":"Systems of equations by substitution","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":80,"prompt":"The numbers <i>a</i>, <i>b</i>, and <i>c</i> satisfy <i>a</i> + <i>b</i> + <i>c</i> = 30, <i>a</i> = 2<i>b</i> − 3, and <i>c</i> = <i>a</i> − 4. What is the value of <i>ac</i>?","nums":[117],"display":"117","explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Write everything in terms of <i>b</i>: <i>a</i> = 2<i>b</i> − 3 and <i>c</i> = <i>a</i> − 4 = 2<i>b</i> − 7.</li><li>Substitute into the sum: (2<i>b</i> − 3) + <i>b</i> + (2<i>b</i> − 7) = 30, so 5<i>b</i> − 10 = 30 and <i>b</i> = 8.</li><li><i>a</i> = 13 and <i>c</i> = 9. Check: 13 + 8 + 9 = 30. ✓</li><li><i>ac</i> = 13 × 9 = <b>117</b>.</li></ol>"},{"n":4,"domain":"Algebra","skill":"Linear equations in two variables","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":80,"prompt":"In the <i>xy</i>-plane, a line has an <i>x</i>-intercept at (−6, 0) and passes through the point (3, 5). Which equation represents the line?","choices":["5<i>x</i> − 9<i>y</i> = −30","9<i>x</i> − 5<i>y</i> = −54","5<i>x</i> + 9<i>y</i> = −30","5<i>x</i> − 9<i>y</i> = 30"],"answer":0,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Slope = (5 − 0) ÷ (3 − (−6)) = 5/9.</li><li>Point-slope form through (−6, 0): <i>y</i> = (5/9)(<i>x</i> + 6).</li><li>Multiply by 9: 9<i>y</i> = 5<i>x</i> + 30, so 5<i>x</i> − 9<i>y</i> = −30.</li><li>Check (3, 5): 15 − 45 = −30. ✓</li></ol>","why":["Correct. Both (−6, 0) and (3, 5) satisfy it.","This uses the reciprocal slope 9/5. It passes through (−6, 0) but not (3, 5): 27 − 25 = 2, not −54.","This has a sign error. It passes through (−6, 0) but at (3, 5) gives 15 + 45 = 60, not −30.","This has its intercept at (6, 0), not (−6, 0)."]},{"n":5,"type":"grid","domain":"Algebra","skill":"Rate problems","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":90,"prompt":"Pipe A can fill an empty tank in 6 hours, and pipe B can fill it in 9 hours. A drain C, working alone, can empty a full tank in 18 hours. If A, B, and C are all open at the same time, how many hours will it take to fill the empty tank?","nums":[4.5],"display":"4.5 (or 9/2)","explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Work in fractions of the tank per hour. A fills 1/6, B fills 1/9, and C removes 1/18.</li><li>Combined rate = 1/6 + 1/9 − 1/18 = 3/18 + 2/18 − 1/18 = 4/18 = 2/9 of the tank per hour.</li><li>Time = 1 ÷ (2/9) = 9/2 = <b>4.5</b> hours.</li></ol>"},{"n":6,"domain":"Algebra","skill":"Inverse of a linear function","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":80,"prompt":"The function <i>f</i> is defined by <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = <span class=\"frac\"><span>2<i>x</i> − 5</span><span>3</span></span> + 1. What is the value of <i>x</i> for which <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = 7 ?","choices":["4","6.5","11.5","13"],"answer":2,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Set (2<i>x</i> − 5)/3 + 1 = 7.</li><li>Subtract 1: (2<i>x</i> − 5)/3 = 6.</li><li>Multiply by 3: 2<i>x</i> − 5 = 18, so 2<i>x</i> = 23 and <i>x</i> = <b>11.5</b>.</li></ol>","why":["This is <i>f</i>(7), the output when the <i>input</i> is 7. The question gives the output 7 and asks for the input.","This comes from a sign error, 2<i>x</i> + 5 = 18, giving <i>x</i> = 6.5.","Correct. <i>f</i>(11.5) = 18/3 + 1 = 7.","This forgets to subtract the +1 first: (2<i>x</i> − 5)/3 = 7 gives <i>x</i> = 13."]},{"n":7,"domain":"Algebra","skill":"Mixture and weighted averages","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":90,"prompt":"A café blends Arabica coffee that costs 18 euros per kilogram with Robusta coffee that costs 11 euros per kilogram. The café makes 35 kilograms of blend that is worth 15 euros per kilogram. How many kilograms of Arabica are in the blend?","choices":["15","20","21","25"],"answer":1,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Let <i>a</i> = kg of Arabica. Then Robusta is 35 − <i>a</i> kg.</li><li>Total cost: 18<i>a</i> + 11(35 − <i>a</i>) = 15(35) = 525.</li><li>18<i>a</i> + 385 − 11<i>a</i> = 525, so 7<i>a</i> = 140 and <i>a</i> = <b>20</b>.</li><li>Check: 20(18) + 15(11) = 360 + 165 = 525. ✓</li></ol>","why":["With 15 kg Arabica the total cost is 15(18) + 20(11) = 490, not 525.","Correct. Total cost is 360 + 165 = 525.","With 21 kg Arabica the total cost is 21(18) + 14(11) = 532, not 525.","With 25 kg Arabica the total cost is 25(18) + 10(11) = 560, not 525."]},{"n":8,"type":"grid","domain":"Algebra","skill":"Systems of linear equations","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":100,"prompt":"The ordered pair (<i>x</i>, <i>y</i>) is the solution to the system 2<i>x</i> + 3<i>y</i> = 19 and 5<i>x</i> − 2<i>y</i> = 0. What is the value of <span class=\"frac\"><span><i>x</i> + <i>y</i></span><span><i>x</i> − <i>y</i></span></span> ?","nums":[-2.3333333333],"tol":0.0006,"display":"−7/3 (or −2.333)","explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>From 5<i>x</i> − 2<i>y</i> = 0, <i>y</i> = 2.5<i>x</i>.</li><li>Substitute into the first equation: 2<i>x</i> + 3(2.5<i>x</i>) = 19, so 9.5<i>x</i> = 19 and <i>x</i> = 2, <i>y</i> = 5.</li><li>Check: 2(2) + 3(5) = 19 ✓ and 5(2) − 2(5) = 0 ✓.</li><li>(<i>x</i> + <i>y</i>)/(<i>x</i> − <i>y</i>) = 7/(−3) = <b>−7/3</b>, which is −2.333 as a decimal.</li></ol>"},{"n":9,"domain":"Advanced Math","skill":"Equivalent expressions","difficulty":"EASY","targetSec":65,"prompt":"Which expression is equivalent to (2<i>x</i> − 3)<sup>2</sup> − (<i>x</i> + 4)(<i>x</i> − 4) ?","choices":["3<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + 25","5<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 12<i>x</i> − 7","3<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 12<i>x</i> − 7","3<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 12<i>x</i> + 25"],"answer":3,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>(2<i>x</i> − 3)<sup>2</sup> = 4<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 12<i>x</i> + 9.</li><li>(<i>x</i> + 4)(<i>x</i> − 4) = <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 16.</li><li>Subtract the whole second expression: 4<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 12<i>x</i> + 9 − <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + 16 = 3<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 12<i>x</i> + 25.</li></ol>","why":["This drops the middle term of the square, treating (2<i>x</i> − 3)<sup>2</sup> as 4<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + 9.","This adds the second product instead of subtracting it.","This subtracts <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> but forgets that subtracting −16 gives +16.","Correct. Subtracting −16 gives +16, so the constant is 9 + 16 = 25."]},{"n":10,"multi":true,"domain":"Advanced Math","skill":"Exponential versus linear growth","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":115,"prompt":"Town A’s population is modelled by <i>A</i>(<i>t</i>) = 800(1.12)<sup><i>t</i></sup> and Town B’s population by <i>B</i>(<i>t</i>) = 3,000 + 150<i>t</i>, where <i>t</i> is the number of whole years since the start of the year 2000. According to the models, in which year does Town A’s population first exceed Town B’s?","choices":["2016","2017","2018","2019"],"answer":2,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>We need the smallest whole number <i>t</i> with 800(1.12)<sup><i>t</i></sup> &gt; 3,000 + 150<i>t</i>. This cannot be solved by ordinary algebra, so compare values or use a graph.</li><li><i>t</i> = 16: <i>A</i> ≈ 4,904 and <i>B</i> = 5,400. <i>A</i> is smaller.</li><li><i>t</i> = 17: <i>A</i> ≈ 5,493 and <i>B</i> = 5,550. <i>A</i> is still smaller (by only about 57).</li><li><i>t</i> = 18: <i>A</i> ≈ 6,152 and <i>B</i> = 5,700. <i>A</i> is now larger.</li><li>So the first year is <i>t</i> = 18, which is the year 2000 + 18 = <b>2018</b>.</li></ol>","desmosTip":"Graph <i>y</i> = 800(1.12)^<i>x</i> and <i>y</i> = 3000 + 150<i>x</i>. The curves cross at <i>x</i> ≈ 17.2, so the first <b>whole</b> year with <i>A</i> larger is 18. Use a table for the two functions to check <i>t</i> = 17 and 18.","why":["In 2016 (<i>t</i> = 16), <i>A</i> ≈ 4,904 is well below <i>B</i> = 5,400.","In 2017 (<i>t</i> = 17), <i>A</i> ≈ 5,493 is still below <i>B</i> = 5,550. The graphs cross just after this year.","Correct. In 2018, <i>A</i> ≈ 6,152 first exceeds <i>B</i> = 5,700.","By 2019 <i>A</i> ≈ 6,890 already exceeds <i>B</i> = 5,850, but 2018 is the <b>first</b> year."]},{"n":11,"multi":true,"type":"grid","domain":"Advanced Math","skill":"Polynomial roots","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":135,"prompt":"Let <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = <i>x</i><sup>3</sup> − 6<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + 5<i>x</i> + 12. The equation <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = 10 has three real solutions. What is the sum of the squares of these three solutions?","nums":[26],"tol":0.05,"display":"26","explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li><i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = 10 is the same as <i>x</i><sup>3</sup> − 6<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + 5<i>x</i> + 2 = 0. Call its solutions <i>p</i>, <i>q</i>, <i>r</i>.</li><li><b>Algebraic route (Vieta’s formulas):</b> for <i>x</i><sup>3</sup> + <i>bx</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>cx</i> + <i>d</i> = 0, the sum of solutions is −<i>b</i> = 6, and the sum of pairwise products is <i>c</i> = 5.</li><li>Use <i>p</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>q</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>r</i><sup>2</sup> = (<i>p</i> + <i>q</i> + <i>r</i>)<sup>2</sup> − 2(<i>pq</i> + <i>pr</i> + <i>qr</i>) = 36 − 10 = <b>26</b>.</li><li><b>Numerical route:</b> the solutions are about −0.2924, 1.3973, and 4.8951. Their squares are about 0.0855 + 1.9525 + 23.962 ≈ 26.0.</li></ol>","desmosTip":"Graph <i>y</i> = <i>x</i>^3 − 6<i>x</i>^2 + 5<i>x</i> + 12 and <i>y</i> = 10. Click the three intersection points to read <i>x</i> ≈ −0.292, 1.397, 4.895. Then type (−0.2924)^2 + (1.3973)^2 + (4.8951)^2 into an expression line. Any answer within 0.05 of 26 is accepted."},{"n":12,"domain":"Advanced Math","skill":"Quadratic functions","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":90,"prompt":"The quadratic function <i>f</i> is defined by <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = <i>a</i>(<i>x</i> − 2)(<i>x</i> + 6), where <i>a</i> is a constant. The graph of <i>y</i> = <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) passes through the point (0, −36). What is the minimum value of <i>f</i>?","choices":["−48","−36","−12","12"],"answer":0,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li><i>f</i>(0) = <i>a</i>(−2)(6) = −12<i>a</i> = −36, so <i>a</i> = 3. The parabola opens upward, so it has a minimum.</li><li>The <i>x</i>-intercepts are 2 and −6, so the vertex has <i>x</i> = (2 + (−6))/2 = −2.</li><li><i>f</i>(−2) = 3(−4)(4) = <b>−48</b>.</li></ol>","why":["Correct. The minimum value is <i>f</i>(−2) = −48.","−36 is the <i>y</i>-intercept, <i>f</i>(0), not the minimum.","−12 is the <i>y</i>-intercept you would get if <i>a</i> were 1. It ignores the given point (0, −36) and is not the minimum.","12 would be the <i>y</i>-intercept if <i>a</i> were −1, and then the parabola would open downward and have no minimum. Here <i>a</i> = 3."]},{"n":13,"type":"grid","domain":"Advanced Math","skill":"Exponential models","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":80,"prompt":"A vehicle loses 15% of its value each year. After 3 years its value is 24,565 euros. What was the vehicle’s original value, in euros?","nums":[40000],"display":"40000","explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Each year the value is multiplied by 0.85. After 3 years: <i>V</i> = <i>V</i><sub>0</sub>(0.85)<sup>3</sup>.</li><li>(0.85)<sup>3</sup> = 0.614125.</li><li><i>V</i><sub>0</sub> = 24,565 ÷ 0.614125 = <b>40,000</b> euros.</li><li>Check: 40,000 × 0.614125 = 24,565. ✓</li></ol>","desmosTip":"Type 24565/0.85^3 into an expression line to read 40000."},{"n":14,"domain":"Advanced Math","skill":"Rational equations","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":115,"prompt":"What is the solution to the equation <span class=\"frac\"><span><i>x</i></span><span><i>x</i> − 2</span></span> + <span class=\"frac\"><span>3</span><span><i>x</i> + 2</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>8</span><span><i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 4</span></span> ?","choices":["−7","−2","2","7"],"answer":0,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Factor the denominator: <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 4 = (<i>x</i> − 2)(<i>x</i> + 2). The common denominator is (<i>x</i> − 2)(<i>x</i> + 2), and <i>x</i> cannot be 2 or −2.</li><li>Multiply through: <i>x</i>(<i>x</i> + 2) + 3(<i>x</i> − 2) = 8.</li><li><i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + 2<i>x</i> + 3<i>x</i> − 6 = 8, so <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + 5<i>x</i> − 14 = 0 and (<i>x</i> + 7)(<i>x</i> − 2) = 0.</li><li>The candidates are −7 and 2. But <i>x</i> = 2 makes two denominators zero, so it is extraneous. The only solution is <b>−7</b>.</li></ol>","why":["Correct. Checking: −7/(−9) + 3/(−5) = 7/9 − 3/5 = 8/45, and 8/(49 − 4) = 8/45. ✓","<i>x</i> = −2 makes the denominators <i>x</i> + 2 and <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 4 zero, so it is not allowed. It is not a root of the quadratic anyway.","<i>x</i> = 2 is a root of the cleared equation but is extraneous, because it makes <i>x</i> − 2 and <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 4 zero.","7 does not satisfy the cleared equation: 49 + 35 − 14 ≠ 0."]},{"n":15,"domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill":"Statistics: effect of an outlier","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":90,"prompt":"Data set A is 4, 6, 8, 10, 12. Data set B is 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 30, which is set A with the value 30 added. Which statement is true?","choices":["The median of B is greater than the mean of B.","Adding 30 increases the mean by more than it increases the median.","The standard deviation of B is less than the standard deviation of A.","The range of B is the same as the range of A."],"answer":1,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Set A: mean = 40/5 = 8, median = 8, range = 12 − 4 = 8.</li><li>Set B: mean = 70/6 ≈ 11.67, median = (8 + 10)/2 = 9, range = 30 − 4 = 26.</li><li>The mean increases by about 3.67 while the median increases by only 1, so the mean is affected more by the added extreme value.</li><li>The other statements fail: the median (9) is less than the mean (11.67); the spread grows, so the standard deviation increases; and the range grows from 8 to 26.</li></ol>","why":["The median of B is 9 and its mean is about 11.67, so the median is less than the mean.","Correct. The mean rises about 3.67 and the median rises 1.","Adding a value far from the rest makes the data more spread out, so the standard deviation increases.","The range increases from 8 to 26."]},{"n":16,"domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill":"Probability without replacement","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":100,"prompt":"A bag contains 5 red, 4 blue, and 3 green marbles that are identical except for colour. Two marbles are drawn at random without replacement. What is the probability that both marbles are the same colour?","choices":["<span class=\"frac\"><span>25</span><span>72</span></span>","<span class=\"frac\"><span>1</span><span>3</span></span>","<span class=\"frac\"><span>19</span><span>144</span></span>","<span class=\"frac\"><span>19</span><span>66</span></span>"],"answer":3,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>There are 12 marbles, so the number of ways to choose 2 is 12 × 11 ÷ 2 = 66.</li><li>Same-colour pairs: red 5 × 4 ÷ 2 = 10, blue 4 × 3 ÷ 2 = 6, green 3 × 2 ÷ 2 = 3. Total 19.</li><li>Probability = 19/66 ≈ 0.288.</li><li>Check with sequential draws: (5/12)(4/11) + (4/12)(3/11) + (3/12)(2/11) = (20 + 12 + 6)/132 = 38/132 = 19/66. ✓</li></ol>","why":["25/72 is the probability if the first marble is <b>replaced</b>: (25 + 16 + 9)/144. The question says without replacement.","1/3 is a rounded guess. The exact probability is 19/66 ≈ 0.288.","19/144 puts the without-replacement count of pairs (19) over the with-replacement total (144).","Correct. 19 favourable pairs out of 66."]},{"n":17,"multi":true,"type":"grid","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill":"Quadratic models from data","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":135,"passage":"<p>A quadratic function of the form <i>y</i> = <i>ax</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>bx</i> + <i>c</i> fits the data in the table exactly.</p>","table":{"caption":"Measured values of y at five values of x","headers":["x","0","2","4","6","8"],"rows":[["y","8","4","4","8","16"]]},"prompt":"According to the model, what is the value of <i>y</i> when <i>x</i> = 11 ?","nums":[35.5],"display":"35.5 (or 71/2)","explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Use three data points to find <i>a</i>, <i>b</i>, <i>c</i>. From (0, 8): <i>c</i> = 8.</li><li>From (2, 4): 4<i>a</i> + 2<i>b</i> + 8 = 4, so 2<i>a</i> + <i>b</i> = −2.</li><li>From (4, 4): 16<i>a</i> + 4<i>b</i> + 8 = 4, so 4<i>a</i> + <i>b</i> = −1.</li><li>Subtract: 2<i>a</i> = 1, so <i>a</i> = 0.5 and <i>b</i> = −3. The model is <i>y</i> = 0.5<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 3<i>x</i> + 8.</li><li>Check with (8, 16): 32 − 24 + 8 = 16. ✓</li><li>At <i>x</i> = 11: 0.5(121) − 33 + 8 = 60.5 − 33 + 8 = <b>35.5</b>.</li></ol>","desmosTip":"Enter the table into Desmos (type <i>x</i><sub>1</sub> and <i>y</i><sub>1</sub> lists, for example [0,2,4,6,8] and [8,4,4,8,16]) and then <i>y</i><sub>1</sub> ~ <i>a</i><i>x</i><sub>1</sub>^2 + <i>b</i><i>x</i><sub>1</sub> + <i>c</i>. Desmos returns <i>a</i> = 0.5, <i>b</i> = −3, <i>c</i> = 8 with zero error. Then evaluate at <i>x</i> = 11."},{"n":18,"domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill":"Study design and inference","difficulty":"MEDIUM","targetSec":80,"prompt":"A researcher selected a random sample of 500 adult residents of a city and recorded how many hours each slept per night and their score on a memory test. Adults who slept more than 8 hours scored higher, on average, than those who slept 8 hours or less. Which conclusion is best supported by this study?","choices":["Among adult residents of this city, more sleep is associated with higher memory scores, but the study does not show that sleep causes the difference.","Sleeping more than 8 hours causes higher memory scores in all adults.","Among all adults in the world, those who sleep more than 8 hours score higher on memory tests.","Adults in the sample who slept 8 hours or less would have scored higher if they had slept longer."],"answer":0,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Two design features decide what can be concluded. The sample was <b>random</b> from the city’s adult residents, so results can be generalized to that population.</li><li>Sleep hours were only <b>observed</b>, not randomly assigned, so the study shows an association, not cause and effect.</li><li>Only the first choice respects both limits.</li></ol>","why":["Correct. Random sampling allows generalization to the city’s adults. No random assignment means no causal claim.","This makes a causal claim, which needs random assignment of sleep, and generalizes beyond the city.","This generalizes to all adults worldwide, but the sample came only from one city.","This is a causal claim about what would have happened. Only random assignment could support it."]},{"n":19,"domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill":"Similar triangles","difficulty":"EASY","targetSec":70,"prompt":"In triangle <i>ABC</i>, point <i>D</i> lies on <i>AB</i> and point <i>E</i> lies on <i>AC</i> so that <i>DE</i> is parallel to <i>BC</i>. <i>AD</i> = 6 cm, <i>DB</i> = 4 cm, and <i>DE</i> = 9 cm. What is the length, in centimetres, of <i>BC</i>?","choices":["6","13","15","22.5"],"answer":2,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Since <i>DE</i> ∥ <i>BC</i>, triangle <i>ADE</i> is similar to triangle <i>ABC</i>.</li><li><i>AB</i> = <i>AD</i> + <i>DB</i> = 6 + 4 = 10.</li><li>Ratio of corresponding sides: <i>DE</i>/<i>BC</i> = <i>AD</i>/<i>AB</i> = 6/10, so 9/<i>BC</i> = 6/10.</li><li><i>BC</i> = 9 × 10 ÷ 6 = <b>15</b> cm.</li></ol>","why":["This uses the ratio <i>AD</i>/<i>DB</i> = 6/4 instead of <i>AD</i>/<i>AB</i> = 6/10 (giving 9 × 4/6 = 6).","This adds <i>DB</i> to <i>DE</i> (9 + 4) instead of using the ratio of similar triangles.","Correct. <i>BC</i> = 9 × (10/6) = 15.","This uses <i>AB</i>/<i>DB</i> = 10/4 as the scale factor (9 × 10/4), but the correct factor is <i>AB</i>/<i>AD</i>."]},{"n":20,"domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill":"Arc length and sector area","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":105,"prompt":"A circular sector has a central angle of <span class=\"frac\"><span>5π</span><span>6</span></span> radians and an arc length of 25 centimetres. What is the area of the sector, in square centimetres?","choices":["<span class=\"frac\"><span>30</span><span>π</span></span>","<span class=\"frac\"><span>375</span><span>π</span></span>","<span class=\"frac\"><span>750</span><span>π</span></span>","375π"],"answer":1,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Arc length = <i>r</i>θ, so 25 = <i>r</i> · (5π/6) and <i>r</i> = 25 × 6/(5π) = 30/π cm.</li><li>Sector area = ½<i>r</i><sup>2</sup>θ = ½ · (30/π)<sup>2</sup> · (5π/6).</li><li>= ½ · (900/π<sup>2</sup>) · (5π/6) = 4,500π/(12π<sup>2</sup>) = <b>375/π</b> cm<sup>2</sup> ≈ 119.4.</li><li>Shortcut: area = ½ × (arc length) × <i>r</i> = ½ × 25 × 30/π = 375/π. ✓</li></ol>","why":["30/π is the <b>radius</b>, not the area.","Correct. Sector area = ½ × arc × radius = 375/π.","750/π forgets the factor ½ in the sector-area formula.","375π drops the π from the denominator of the radius, treating <i>r</i> as 30 instead of 30/π."]},{"n":21,"domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill":"Trigonometric functions of an angle","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":105,"prompt":"For an angle θ, cos θ = −<span class=\"frac\"><span>4</span><span>5</span></span> and π &lt; θ &lt; <span class=\"frac\"><span>3π</span><span>2</span></span>. What is the value of sin θ + tan θ ?","choices":["−<span class=\"frac\"><span>27</span><span>20</span></span>","−<span class=\"frac\"><span>3</span><span>20</span></span>","<span class=\"frac\"><span>3</span><span>20</span></span>","<span class=\"frac\"><span>27</span><span>20</span></span>"],"answer":2,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Angles with π &lt; θ &lt; 3π/2 lie in the third quadrant, where sine and cosine are both negative and tangent is positive.</li><li>sin<sup>2</sup>θ = 1 − cos<sup>2</sup>θ = 1 − 16/25 = 9/25, so sin θ = −3/5 (negative in this quadrant).</li><li>tan θ = sin θ ÷ cos θ = (−3/5) ÷ (−4/5) = 3/4.</li><li>sin θ + tan θ = −3/5 + 3/4 = −12/20 + 15/20 = <b>3/20</b>.</li></ol>","why":["This takes tan θ as negative (−3/4) as well, but tangent is positive in the third quadrant.","This takes sin θ as positive (+3/5) and tan θ as negative, which is the wrong quadrant’s signs.","Correct. −3/5 + 3/4 = 3/20.","This takes both sin θ and tan θ as positive, but sine is negative in the third quadrant."]},{"n":22,"domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill":"Circles: tangent lines","difficulty":"HARD","targetSec":115,"prompt":"In the <i>xy</i>-plane, a circle has its centre at (−2, 3) and passes through the point (1, 7). Which equation represents the line that is tangent to the circle at the point (1, 7)?","choices":["4<i>x</i> − 3<i>y</i> = −17","3<i>x</i> − 4<i>y</i> = −25","4<i>x</i> + 3<i>y</i> = 25","3<i>x</i> + 4<i>y</i> = 31"],"answer":3,"explanation":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li>A tangent line is perpendicular to the radius at the point of tangency. The radius from (−2, 3) to (1, 7) has slope (7 − 3)/(1 − (−2)) = 4/3.</li><li>The tangent slope is the negative reciprocal: −3/4.</li><li>Through (1, 7): <i>y</i> − 7 = −(3/4)(<i>x</i> − 1). Multiply by 4: 4<i>y</i> − 28 = −3<i>x</i> + 3, so 3<i>x</i> + 4<i>y</i> = 31.</li><li>Check (1, 7): 3 + 28 = 31. ✓</li></ol>","why":["This is the line through the centre and (1, 7), the <b>radius</b> (slope 4/3), not the tangent.","This has slope 3/4, the reciprocal of the radius slope but with the wrong sign.","This has slope −4/3, the negative of the radius slope. The tangent needs the negative <b>reciprocal</b>, −3/4.","Correct. Slope −3/4 through (1, 7)."]}]}}};
